AB  - Aim. Based on a modular approach, this study provides a comparative analysis of the functional status and reserve capabilities of the cardiorespiratory system in adolescent water polo athletes, swimmers, and their untrained peers. Material and methods. The study involved three groups of adolescent males (n = 40 per group; mean age 13.7 ± 1.23 years): water polo players, middle-distance swimmers, and school children whose physical activity was limited to the standard curriculum. A modular approach was implemented in a three-stage structure of the study: Stage 1 – Respiration; Stage 2 – Circulation; Stage 3 – Integral cardiorespiratory performance. Results. Water polo players and swimmers demonstrated statistically significant (p < 0.05) superiority over their untrained peers in all the parameters under study: vital capacity, hypoxic resistance, and aerobic and anaerobic endurance. No statistically significant differences were observed between athletic groups for the majority of parameters, except for a tendency towards greater anaerobic endurance in water polo players. Conclusion. Systematic training in water sports leads to a pronounced and similar increase in the functional reserves of the cardiorespiratory system in adolescents, reflecting a powerful aerobic adaptation to the environment. Minor differences between the athletes may be explained by the specific nature of their training load. In particular, water polo places higher demands on the glycolytic component of endurance. The battery of methods used is informative for both screening assessment and monitoring during the preparatory training period. © Петрушкина Н.П., Звягина Е.В., Латюшин Я.В., 2026.
