DO  - 10.16156/j.1004-7220.2026.04.036
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048400796?origin=resultslist
AB  - Objective To examine the energy metabolic characteristics and heart rate responses of elite Chinese Paralympic cross-country skiers of different functional classifications under snow tunnel environment, so as to reveal the relationship between lactate kinetics and skiing performance. Methods Fourteen elite athletes from the Chinese National Paralympic Cross-Country Skiing Team participated in this study. Based on functional classification, participants were divided into a standing group (LW5-8, n = 7) and a sitting group (LW11-12, n = 7). Incremental load skiing tests were conducted in a snow tunnel, and blood lactate concentration and heart rate data were collected. The Shapiro-Wilk and Levene tests were used to assess data normality and homogeneity of variance. Between-group differences were analyzed using independent-samples t-tests or Mann-Whitney U tests. Partial correlation analysis was performed to explore the relationships between lactate kinetics and heart rate recovery parameters. A simple linear regression analysis was conducted with adjusted time (tadj) as the dependent variable and lactate rise slope (KBLa) as the independent variable. Results The standing group exhibited significantly lower tadj and KBLa values than the sitting group (P < 0.05), whereas the mean heart rate response (ΔHR_mean), mean 60-second heart rate recovery (HRR60-mean), and normalized recovery (HRR%60-mean) were significantly higher in the standing group (P < 0.05). The tadj and KBLa showed a strong negative correlation (r = -0.734, P = 0.004), and the regression equation was tadj = 4488.748 - 742059.087 × KBLa (R2 = 0.750, P < 0.001). The sitting group demonstrated a higher within-group regression fit (R2 = 0.500) than the standing group (R2 = 0.013). Conclusions Paralympic cross-country skiers of different functional classifications displayed distinct characteristics in energy metabolism and heart rate recovery. The rate of lactate accumulation can serve as a key monitoring indicator reflecting metabolic regulation capacity and skiing performance. Training strategies should be tailored to functional type: standing athletes should strengthen aerobic metabolism and lactate clearance, whereas sitting athletes should enhance oxidative metabolism and lactate tolerance in the upper limb and core muscles. This study provides scientific evidence to guide training monitoring and performance optimization for Paralympic cross-country skiers. © 2026, Editorial Department of Journal of Shanghai Second Medical University. All rights reserved.
