TI  - A Study on NLMS-Based Adaptive ECG Filtering Algorithm Assisted by Dual-Channel IMU Signals
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 6th International Symposium on Artificial Intelligence for Medical Sciences, ISAIMS 2025
SP  - 262
EP  - 267
DO  - 10.1145/3777577.3777619
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105028508463?origin=resultslist
AB  - In wearable electrocardiogram (ECG) monitoring systems, dynamic motion environments often introduce disturbances such as baseline drift and transient motion artifacts, which severely compromise signal quality and the accuracy of rhythm feature extraction. To address this issue, this study proposes an online denoising algorithm based on the Normalized Least Mean Square (NLMS) adaptive filtering framework with dual-reference inputs. The algorithm utilizes inertial measurement units (IMUs) as auxiliary sources, from which a velocity signal is derived via integration of X-axis acceleration, and a quaternion real part is obtained through fusion of tri-axial angular velocity. These two reference signals are concatenated into a vector and used as inputs to the NLMS filter, enabling joint modeling and suppression of both low-frequency baseline drift and high-frequency motion artifacts. To evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm, a multimodal dataset was constructed, covering eight typical daily activities, including deep breathing, squatting, chest expansion, arm lifting, and running. Performance was assessed using metrics such as baseline power reduction (BPR), mean square error (MSE), residual baseline amplitude (R-BL), R-peak retention rate, RR interval deviation (IRR), and Pearson correlation coefficient (PCC). Experimental results demonstrate that in the deep breathing scenario, the algorithm achieved a BPR of 33.98 dB, MSE of, 100% R-peak retention rate, and a PCC of 0.9355. Comparable artifact suppression and signal fidelity were observed in other scenarios as well. Compared to traditional approaches requiring dynamic channel switching or complex signal decomposition, the proposed algorithm offers a simpler structure and higher computational efficiency, making it well-suited for real-time deployment in wearable ECG monitoring under dynamic conditions. It holds significant potential for practical application.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
