TI  - Social representation of palliative care in Brazil: A comparison of healthcare professionals of the public health system with and without training
PY  - 2026
T2  - Palliative Care and Social Practice
VL  - 20
DO  - 10.1177/26323524261473940
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053775359?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background: Despite the growth of palliative care (PC) in Brazil, research on social representations is largely fragmented and localized, hindering a comprehensive understanding of professional perceptions across the public healthcare system. Objectives: This study investigated the effect of palliative care training in the social representation (SR) of PC among healthcare professionals (HCP). It analyzed the differences of SR considering over or under 180 hours of PC training and the effect of 20 hours PC training compared to those without training. Design: A descriptive-exploratory study used free word evocation to assess SR of PC using cross-sectional and longitudinal components. Methods: Subjects were asked to write down five words prompted by: “when you hear the term palliative care, which words or expressions come immediately to mind?”. SR were analyzed by word frequency and mentioned rank (relevance). Then, a long-distance 20 hours PC training was provided and to these professionals the SR was remade. Results: 2.417 HCP responded. From 7.205 evoked words clustered into 19 elements, the untrained group’s central core (high frequency and relevance) included “comfort” and “care”. The trained group included “comfort”, “care”, “dignity”, “acolhimento” (holding space), and “quality of life”. As for the secondary analysis, before training, participants evoked “analgesia”, “death” and “assistance” while post-training responses included “integrality”, “autonomy” and “team”. Conclusions: HCPs with more PC training demonstrated different SR structures, shifting from passive, commonsense views to technical-active perspectives. Their SR focused more on life than death, contrasting with less-trained colleagues. Even brief training programs were associated to meaningful changes. © The Author(s) 2026. This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) which permits non-commercial use, reproduction and distribution of the work without further permission provided the original work is attributed as specified on the SAGE and Open Access pages (https://us.sagepub.com/en-us/nam/open-access-at-sage).
