TI  - Application Research of a Random Forest-Based Hybrid Problem-Based Learning (PBL) Model in College Swimming Rescue Courses
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 International Conference on Digital Transformation and Management, DTM 2025
SP  - 273
EP  - 281
DO  - 10.1145/3795546.3795585
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038085745?origin=resultslist
AB  - This study investigates the effectiveness of integrating a learning analytics approach based on Random Forest (RF) into a blended problem-based learning (PBL) framework for university swimming lifeguard courses. This framework combines blended online-offline learning, scenario-based PBL tasks, and machine learning-driven analysis to enhance students’ rescue skills and emergency decision-making abilities. A total of 90 undergraduate students participated in the 15-week instructional intervention and were randomly assigned to either the experimental or control group. Throughout the course, we collected multidimensional data encompassing swimming performance metrics, technical and scenario assessment scores, learning behavior indicators, and training load variables. A Random Forest regression model was constructed to identify key predictors of rescue performance, with feature importance extracted for pedagogical interpretation. Results demonstrated that experimental group students achieved significantly greater progress than control group students across core rescue performance metrics, including 25-meter lifesaving swim time, 15-meter tow rescue time, technical execution scores, and scenario-based rescue performance (p<0.001). Feature importance analysis indicated that tow rescue efficiency, swimming speed, and scenario-based assessment were the most significant predictors of rescue effectiveness, while learning behavior variables (e.g., PBL task completion and discussion participation) also contributed significantly to overall rescue outcomes. These findings demonstrate that integrating learning analytics based on random forests with blended PBL teaching methods effectively enhances students’ technical proficiency, practical judgment, and applied rescue skills in swimming rescue education. The proposed framework provides empirical evidence for data-driven teaching optimization in safety-critical physical education courses related to life preservation. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
