TI  - On the Optimal Number of Training Sessions for Long Distance Running
PY  - 2027
T2  - Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems
VL  - 2146 LNNS
SP  - 202
EP  - 219
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-35078-7_12
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052383877?origin=resultslist
AB  - Running, particularly long-distance running, has experienced a substantial rise in global participation over the past decade. Recreational and competitive athletes alike increasingly participate in these events due to their accessibility, cardiovascular benefits and community-driven appeal. However, this growth has also been accompanied by a heightened prevalence of running-related injuries, most of which are attributed to overuse and excessive training loads. Endurance athletes must, therefore, balance training frequency and volume in a way that maximizes performance while minimizing injury risk. The purpose of this study is to determine the optimal number of weekly training sessions for long-distance runners that allows for performance maximization while maintaining a low probability of injury. Using a longitudinal dataset of competitive runners, this study applies an injury-adjusted performance model to identify a training session frequency that optimizes weekly running output. Findings indicate that while injury risk increases beyond six to seven weekly sessions, performance-adjusted outcomes peak at approximately twelve sessions per week, suggesting that carefully managed higher-frequency training may be beneficial for certain athletes. Additionally, it was determined that injuries are a complex phenomena with many factors contributing to injury risk. Main drivers were found to be the exertion of athletes particularly in the week leading up to the injury and major decelerators of injury were found to be the rest days an athlete takes in the weeks prior. These results provide valuable insights for coaches, athletes and sports practitioners seeking evidence-based guidance for training prescription. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
