TI  - A Computational Study on the Effects of Menstrual Cycle on Training Performance and Fatigue Perception in Female Artistic Swimmers
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2026 6th International Conference on Biomedicine and Bioinformatics Engineering, ICBBE 2026
SP  - 652
EP  - 658
DO  - 10.1145/3822183.3822284
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052734662?origin=resultslist
AB  - Objective: To investigate the effects of different menstrual cycle phases on subjective fatigue perception and training performance in female artistic swimmers using computational data mining and predictive modeling approaches. Computational Methods: Twenty first-line female artistic swimmers (aged 16-24 years) were recruited. Daily recordings of menstrual cycle, RPE (0-10), coach technical score (1-5), and a menstrual symptom questionnaire (0-12) were collected over two cycles. Cycles were divided into four phases. Computational analysis pipeline included: (1) descriptive statistics and ANOVA using SPSS; (2) Pearson correlation-based feature selection to identify key symptom predictors; (3) construction of linear regression models for predicting RPE and training performance from symptom scores; (4) evaluation using machine learning metrics (R2, RMSE, MAE) and leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV). Results: RPE was significantly higher in menstrual vs. follicular phase (7.2±1.1 vs. 5.8±0.9, p<0.01). Training performance was lowest in menstrual phase (3.1±0.5, p<0.01). The linear regression model predicting RPE from symptom score achieved R2=0.52, RMSE=0.48, MAE=0.39 under LOOCV; the model for training performance achieved R2=0.46, RMSE=0.31, MAE=0.25. Conclusion: Computational analysis reveals strong associations between menstrual symptoms, fatigue, and performance. The developed predictive models provide a data-driven foundation for individualized training adjustments.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
