TI  - Precision exercise prescription: a review integrating autonomic nervous system monitoring and machine learning to optimize VO2max adaptation for health and performance
PY  - 2026
T2  - Sport Sciences for Health
VL  - 22
IS  - 4
C7  - 362
DO  - 10.1007/s11332-026-01949-6
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052011364?origin=resultslist
AB  - Purpose: Individual adaptation to standardized aerobic training exhibits significant variability, with approximately 15% of participants identified as non-responders regarding maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) gains. This paper investigates how monitoring the autonomic nervous system (ANS) through heart rate variability (HRV) can serve as a non-invasive, practical tool to personalize exercise prescriptions, improve training responsiveness, and enhance cardiovascular health. Methods: The study synthesizes evidence from over 40 controlled trials and meta-analyses. It evaluates classical HRV metrics, such as LnRMSSD and the LF/HF ratio, alongside emerging methodologies including detrended fluctuation analysis (DFA-α1) and AI-powered multisensory fusion involving electrodermal activity and skin temperature. Results: Findings indicate that early HRV shifts during the first week of training are moderate predictors of long-term VO2max adaptation. HRV-guided protocols lead to more uniform VO2max gains and fewer non-responders compared to fixed-intensity training. Furthermore, advanced metrics and machine learning models offer more transparent, context-aware insights into physiological readiness than traditional linear analysis. Conclusions: Transitioning from “one-size-fits-all” models to ANS-guided, population-stratified prescriptions are essential for maximizing the preventive benefits of exercise. Integrating wearable technology and explainable AI into clinical practice provides a scalable framework for improving functional capacity and cardiovascular health across diverse populations. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag Italia S.r.l., part of Springer Nature 2026.
