TI  - Longer 24-Hour Total Sleep Time During High-Volume Training in Collegiate Endurance Runners: A Longitudinal Field Study Using a Non-Contact Sheet-Type Sleep Monitor
PY  - 2026
T2  - Sleep and Vigilance
DO  - 10.1007/s41782-026-00376-4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051574230?origin=resultslist
AB  - Purpose: Sleep is a key component of recovery and adaptation in endurance athletes, yet sleep recommendations are often communicated as a fixed nightly target despite substantial fluctuations in training load. This study examined whether 24-h total sleep time, including daytime sleep episodes, differed across distinct training phases in collegiate endurance runners. Methods: Twelve male collegiate long-distance runners were monitored in their usual training environment during an intensive training-camp phase (24 days), a recovery phase between camps (17 days), and a competition phase during the academic term (30 days). Sleep was assessed using a non-contact sheet-type sleep monitor, and daily running distance from coaching logs was used as the indicator of training volume. Results: Mean daily running distance was highest during the intensive phase (31.74 ± 12.63 km/day) compared with the recovery (19.37 ± 11.84 km/day) and competition (18.67 ± 10.48 km/day) phases (both p < 0.01). Daily 24-h total sleep time was longer during the intensive phase (525.36 ± 25.45 min/day) than during the recovery (452.62 ± 42.36 min/day; difference 72.74 min/day, p < 0.05) and competition (454.09 ± 44.68 min/day; difference 71.27 min/day, p < 0.01) phases. Sleep latency was also longer during the intensive phase than during the competition phase (31.60 ± 6.31 versus 17.55 ± 4.76 min; p < 0.01). Total sleep time did not differ between the recovery and competition phases. Conclusions: In these collegiate endurance runners, the highest-volume training phase was associated with a marked extension of 24-h total sleep time. The near-identical sleep duration observed during the recovery and competition phases—despite their differing environmental and competitive contexts—supports training load as the primary correlate of total daily sleep. These findings build on prior field observations from our group and highlight the value of evaluating total daily sleep rather than nocturnal sleep alone. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.
