UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043704944?origin=resultslist
AB  - Purpose: To investigate the kinetics of a non-linear heart rate (HR) variability parameter (DFA α1) during submaximal cycling in male and female trained and highly trained cyclists in a non-fatigued state and after repeated maximal cycling bouts. Methods: N = 47 participants (17 female) completed a cycling protocol consisting of two blocks (Block1, Block2), each including two standardised submaximal cycling bouts performed prior (PRE) and post a maximal incremental test (POST), having four time points (Block1: PRE1-POST1; Block2: PRE2-POST2). DFA α1, oxygen consumption (), HR, respiratory frequency (RF) and rating of perceived exertion (RPE) were assessed during the submaximal bouts, and peak and maximal power output (Wmax) during the maximal tests. A linear mixed effects model was used to analyse data. Results: Block2 showed significantly lower peak (β = − 2.51 mL·kg⁻¹·min⁻¹) and Wmax (β = − 24.3 W) values. DFA α1 decreased significantly from PRE1 (1.32 ± 0.19) to POST1 (1.12 ± 0.34) and remained suppressed at Block2 (1.14 ± 0.31, 1.09 ± 0.35). (+ 2.37 mL·kg⁻1·min⁻1), HR (+ 15 bpm), RF (+ 8 breaths/min) and RPE (+ 2) increased significantly in Block1 and stayed elevated in Block2. Following POST2, HR decreased (–4 bpm) and RF increased (+ 1 breaths/min) significantly. Training status significantly influenced absolute levels of DFA α1, HR, and, but did not influence RPE or RF. Sex did not significantly alter kinetics of DFA α1, HR, V̇O₂, RPE and RF. Conclusion: DFA α1 during submaximal exercise reflects both acute fatigue and non-recovered state in a repeated maximal test setting and may provide complementary information on autonomic and systemic demands when interpreted alongside, HR, RF and RPE. © The Author(s) 2026.
