TI  - Effect of dry needling on thoracic and lumbar extensor muscle tone and perceived recovery in elite cross-country skiers: A sham-controlled randomized crossover pilot trial
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Bodywork and Movement Therapies
VL  - 48
SP  - 1077
EP  - 1083
DO  - 10.1016/j.jbmt.2026.08.023
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047518957?origin=resultslist
AB  - Objective To estimate the short-term effects of dry needling, compared with sham needling, on thoracic and lumbar extensor mechanical muscle properties and perceived recovery in elite cross-country skiers. Methods This randomized crossover pilot trial included 10 athletes (mean age: 21 ± 2 years; body mass index: 22.7 ± 1.4; 80% men) across two 10-day pre-season training camps separated by approximately two months. Participants received two dry needling sessions during one camp and two sham needling sessions during the other, according to a randomized sequence. MyotonPRO measurements of frequency, stiffness, and elasticity were obtained before, immediately after, and 48 h after each treatment session. Perceived recovery was assessed using the Short Recovery and Stress Scale on days 0, 5, and 10 of each camp. Repeated-measures mixed models examined intervention, time, and intervention-by-time effects. Results No significant main effects of intervention were observed for the mechanical muscle properties or perceived recovery. A significant intervention-by-time interaction was observed for thoracic elasticity (p = 0.03), although post hoc comparisons were nonsignificant. Significant main effects of time were observed for lumbar frequency (p = 0.01), lumbar elasticity (p < 0.01), and perceived recovery (p < 0.01). Lumbar frequency and logarithmic decrement increased transiently after treatment, while perceived recovery declined during the training camps. Conclusion In this sham-controlled crossover pilot study, estimates did not show a clear advantage of dry needling over sham needling for thoracic or lumbar mechanical muscle properties or perceived recovery. The imprecision of the estimates precludes conclusions about smaller potentially meaningful effects. © 2026 The Authors.
