TI  - Optimal HIIT for Aerobic, Anaerobic, Performance and Metabolic Responses: A Systematic Critical Review and Meta-Analysis
ST  - HIIT óptimo Para Respuestas Aeróbicas, Anaeróbicas, de Rendimiento y Metabólicas: Una Revisión Crítica Sistemática y un Metanálisis
PY  - 2026
T2  - Archivos de Medicina del Deporte
VL  - 42
IS  - 6
SP  - 296
EP  - 306
DO  - 10.18176/ARCHMEDDEPORTE.00225
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048722292?origin=resultslist
AB  - Introduction: This systematic critical review and meta-analysis aims to identify the most effective High-Intensity Interval Training (HIIT) protocols for endurance athletes, focusing on enhancing aerobic and anaerobic capacities, performance, perceptual responses, and metabolic parameters. Addressing a significant knowledge gap, the study investigates the doseresponse relationship between HIIT training loads and their adaptations. Material and method: It involved a comprehensive search across five databases—Web of Science, Scopus, PubMed, Cochrane and PEDro, including an in-depth review of citations, references, Google Scholar and grey literature up to December 2022. The emphasis was on studies assessing the impact of HIIT on aerobic parameters (like maximal and peak oxygen consumption, respiratory exchange ratio, maximum and peak heart rates), performance, anaerobic capabilities (peak and mean power output), perceptual responses, and metabolic indicators, particularly blood lactate levels. Results: Out of 1950 studies, 10 were chosen, comparing 21 HIIT protocols. Results indicated that a 1:8 sprint interval training significantly improves maximal oxygen consumption (VO2max) within four weeks. Traditional-HIIT with 2:1, 1:1, and 1:9 work: recovery ratios over 3, 4, and 10-weeks, respectively, also effectively enhanced VO2max. A 2:1 HIIT protocol over four weeks showed notable improvements in peak oxygen consumption (VO2peak). Additionally, the 1:8 sprint interval training was particularly effective in improving power outputs, performance metrics, and blood lactate. Conclusions: The study highlights that different HIITs, when appropriately customized, can improve anaerobic capacity, performance, and metabolic parameters in athletes. It concludes that sprint interval training may offer more substantial anaerobic benefits compared to traditional-HIIT approaches. © (2026), (Archivos de Medicina del Deporte). All rights reserved.
