TI  - Cardiorespiratory coupling tightens with workload during graded exercise: A pilot study with adolescent athletes
PY  - 2026
T2  - Physiological Reports
VL  - 14
IS  - 8
C7  - e70884
DO  - 10.14814/phy2.70884
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036811283?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cardiorespiratory coupling (CRC) reflects coordination between heart and lung function, but how it changes with increasing intensity during graded exercise remains unclear. We investigated CRC as real-time covariation between cardiac timing and breath-by-breath pulmonary oxygen uptake (VO2) to test whether coupling strengthens with workload in adolescent athletes. We conducted an observational, within-subject analysis of the ACTES cycling dataset. Eighteen adolescents cycled at 50, 110, and 140 W. Beat-to-beat RR intervals and pulmonary VO2 time series were examined in ultra-short 60 s segments. Fluctuations were summarized using SDRR/RMSSD and SDVO2/RMSSDVO2. CRC was measured using joint symbolic dynamics (JSD; Shannon entropy, SE, and Miller–Madow–corrected entropy, CSE) and the highest normalized cross-correlation (X-Corr). Mean RR decreased and mean pulmonary VO2 increased with workload (both p < 0.0001). SDRR and RMSSD were lower at 110 and 140 W versus 50 W; SDVO2 declined from 50 to 110/140 W, whereas RMSSDVO2 was unchanged. X-Corr increased from 50 to 110/140 W (p ≤ 0.0014). JSD indices decreased as workload increased (SE: global p = 0.139; CSE: global p = 0.029), suggesting tighter CRC. CRC becomes more pronounced with increased workload, aligning with reduced heart rate variability and reflecting vagal withdrawal and reflex responses that improve heart–lung integration in adolescent athletes. © 2026 The Author(s). Physiological Reports published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of The Physiological Society and the American Physiological Society.
