TI  - Experimental study on the cyclic tensile mechanical performance of large-diameter 7 × 19 superelastic NiTi shape memory alloy cables
PY  - 2027
T2  - Journal of Constructional Steel Research
VL  - 248
C7  - 110709
DO  - 10.1016/j.jcsr.2026.110709
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051374053?origin=resultslist
AB  - The uniaxial cyclic tensile mechanical performance and superelastic response of large-diameter (15.0 mm) 7 × 19 superelastic NiTi shape memory alloy (SMA) cables at ambient temperature were experimentally investigated. The study systematically examined the effects of annealing duration, strain amplitude, number of cycles, pre-training, loading rate, and pre-strain on key mechanical indicators. Specimens were annealed at 400 °C for durations ranging from 10 to 50 min. Loading protocols included cyclic loading with incrementally increasing strain amplitudes, constant-amplitude cyclic loading, incremental loading after pre-training, and pre-strained loading paths. Results show that the cables display typical flag-shaped hysteresis over a 10% strain range. The phase transformation plateaus are smoother and occur at lower stress than those of the corresponding SMA wires. Annealing duration strongly affects the phase transformation plateaus and strength retention. As annealing time increases, plateau stresses and peak stress decrease, while residual strain rises and recoverability worsens. Optimal performance occurs with annealing at 400 °C for 10 min. Under constant-amplitude cycling, plateau stresses and energy dissipation degrade rapidly during the first three cycles before stabilizing, with larger strain amplitudes accelerating the degradation and residual strain buildup. Pre-training reduces residual strain, enhances recoverability, and improves strength and stiffness retention when the applied strain exceeds the pre-training level. Increasing the loading rate within the quasi-static range raises both forward and reverse transformation plateaus but reduces energy dissipation. The reverse plateau is more sensitive to loading rate. Pre-strain increases initial stiffness and load-bearing capacity. However, under unidirectional pre-strain, restoring stress decreases as working strain increases. Bidirectional pre-strain helps retain strength and stiffness and reduces residual strain at higher strain levels. These findings provide useful guidance for performance evaluation and key parameter selection of large-diameter SMA cables under various loading conditions. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
