TI  - Cardiovascular and autonomic responses to exercise under different temperature and humidity conditions in young males
PY  - 2026
T2  - Sports Medicine and Health Science
DO  - 10.1016/j.smhs.2026.05.003
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043360074?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background: This study examined the effects of different temperature and humidity conditions on cardiovascular responses and autonomic nervous regulation during a 5-km running trial in young males. Methods: Ten participants completed the running trials under three environmental conditions in a randomized order: normal temperature and humidity (NN, [23 ± 1] °C; Relative humidity = 40%), high temperature normal humidity (HN, [35 ± 1] °C; Relative humidity = 40%), and high temperature high humidity (HH, [35 ± 1] °C; Relative humidity = 80%). Heart rate (HR), heart rate variability (HRV), gastrointestinal temperature (Tgi), skin temperature (Tsk), and physiological strain index (PSI) were measured before, during, and after exercise. Blood pressure (BP), sweat rate (SR), rate-pressure product (RPP), serum epinephrine (EPI) and norepinephrine (NE) levels were monitored pre- and post-exercise. Results: Compared with the NN and HN conditions, participants exhibited a markedly extended 5-km running time under the HH condition (p < 0.05). Tgi was highest in HH during exercise (p < 0.05). At 5-km, HR was higher in HN and HH than NN (p < 0.05); post-exercise diastolic BP was lower in HN than NN (p < 0.05), and RPP was higher in HH and HN than NN (p < 0.05). SR was greater in HN and HH than NN after 5-km run (p < 0.05). The average Low Frequency/High Frequency (LF/HF) over 5-km was higher in HH than NN (p < 0.05). Post-exercise serum NE increased in HH, and EPI elevated in HN and HH compared with baseline and NN (p < 0.05). Conclusions: These results indicate that high temperature and high humidity environments increase cardiovascular and autonomic stress during exercise, characterized by sympathetic overactivation coexisting with uncoordinated withdrawal of vagal activity, potentially elevating the risk of cardiovascular events. © 2026 Publishing services by Elsevier B.V. on behalf of KeAi Communications Co. Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
