TI  - Acute effects of slow- and fast-paced breathing on endocrine, autonomic and psychological outcomes following intense physical exertion in healthy recreational athletes
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Journal of Sport and Exercise Psychology
DO  - 10.1080/1612197X.2026.2730162
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050293971?origin=resultslist
AB  - Objectives: This study investigated slow-paced breathing (SPB) and fast-paced breathing (FPB) effects on endocrine, autonomic, and psychological markers following intense physical exertion and whether FPB enhances running performance. We hypothesised that SPB (post-exertion) and FPB (pre-exertion) would attenuate the stress response and FPB would improve running performance. Design: In a randomised crossover design, 18 male athletes performed three all-out five-minutes runs. Cortisol, alpha-amylase, cardiac vagal activity (RMSSD), subjective stress, and emotional response were measured at: baseline, post-exertion, post-breathing, and after 10-minute rest. Results: Physical exertion increased cortisol, alpha-amylase, and subjective stress, while reducing RMSSD and shifting emotional response toward more negative, high-arousal emotional state. SPB showed lower cortisol in post-hoc analyses, though mixed-effects models (LMM) did not confirm this. FPB acutely increased subjective stress post-intervention and improved subsequent running performance. Following the 10-minute rest, FPB was associated with lower cortisol and elevated RMSSD values. Conclusion: SPB and FPB elicited distinct psychophysiological profiles. While SPB’s inconsistent effects preclude definitive recovery conclusions, FPB functioned as a potent stress modulator. Immediate subjective stress increase and enhanced running performance under FPB were contrasted by lower cortisol and higher cardiac vagal activity emerging after the 10-minute rest. This response may reflect suppressed HPA-axis activity and compensatory vagal rebound, potentially intensified by greater cumulative physiological load of improved running performance. Notably, these effects cannot be isolated from exertion itself, as enhanced running performance constitutes a confounding factor. Given high inter-individual variability in running performance outcomes, FPB should be a tailored pre-activation strategy to optimise physical exertion. © 2026 International Society of Sport Psychology.
