AB  - Sport science plays a critical role in guiding athletic performance improvement through data-driven decisions. This chapter presents evidence that sport science, when effectively integrated, provides crucial insights into physical capabilities, recovery and training efficiency. This chapter covers practical considerations for using performance assessments and athlete monitoring systems to optimize decision-making in collaboration with coaches, athletes and performance staff. Specifically, data on strength, power and conditioning are shown to serve as reliable indicators of performance readiness and potential adaptations. Case studies are presented to illustrate how normative strength values across sports, seasonal strength changes in NCAA rowing and multi-year strength development in collegiate baseball correlate with performance outcomes. These examples highlight the importance of using consistent data to drive training adjustments and improve performance. Based on the findings, it is recommended that regular performance monitoring be incorporated into training programs to support decision making and maximize athletic development. This chapter reinforces the value of a systematic, collaborative approach to sport science in supporting long-term performance success. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Paul Comfort and Paul A. Jones; individual chapters, the contributors.
