TI  - CarbonTrackAI: Real-Time Carbon Footprint Estimation from Daily Activities
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Intelligent and Innovative Technologies in Computing, Electrical and Electronics, IITCEE 2026
DO  - 10.1109/IITCEE67948.2026.11394264
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105039312220?origin=resultslist
AB  - Personal carbon accounting is hampered by fragmented data streams (mobility, electricity, food), nonstandard emission factors, and privacy risks that deter the sharing of raw personal data. We propose CarbonTrackAI, a privacy-preserving, multimodal system that estimates an individual’s near–real-time carbon footprint (CF) from everyday activities by fusing smartphone sensing (for mobility and activity), smart-meter and appliance disaggregation (for household energy), and purchase/meal logs (for food). CarbonTrackAI combines on-device activity recognition to derive trip segments and transport modes, non-intrusive load monitoring (NILM) to apportion household electricity to end-uses, recipe/food databases and hybrid LCA factors to quantify diet emissions; and carbon-intensity signals of the local grid to translate energy into CO2e dynamically. A privacy layer uses configurable DP-SGD and secure aggregation for optional model improvement without exposing raw data. The design is motivated by recent evidence: deep learning has advanced NILM and appliance-level inference in homes; transport-mode detection on smartphones routinely exceeds 90% accuracy; electricity grid carbon intensity can be modeled and forecast with high spatiotemporal fidelity; carbon-tracking apps and personalized feedback can reduce reported emissions for at least some users in the short term. The paper proposes a harmonization layer that maps heterogeneous inputs to a common schema aligned with recognized LCA sources for food and with regional grid-intensity feeds for energy, a transparent explainability & audit interface that surfaces factors, sources, and confidence intervals per estimate; and an evaluation protocol spanning accuracy (vs. ground-truth meters, GPS logs, and annotated receipts), calibration, latency, robustness to missing data, and user-centric metrics (readability, actionability). CarbonTrackAI targets households and individuals, clinicians and digital-health programs (quantifying lifestyle interventions), and sustainability teams running behavioral pilots. By emphasizing privacy-preserving learning and actionable feedback, CarbonTrackAI bridges the gap between academic estimators and deployable, user-trusted tools. ©2026 IEEE.
