TI  - Real-Time Performance Prediction for Track Cycling Using LSTM: A Comparative Study with MLP and ANFIS
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems
VL  - 1897 LNNS
SP  - 368
EP  - 374
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-20722-7_47
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045133949?origin=resultslist
AB  - Accurate performance prediction in track cycling is a challenging time-series problem involving interacting physiological and biomechanical variables. This study compares three artificial intelligence approaches—Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, Multilayer Perceptron (MLP), and Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System (ANFIS)—for real-time prediction of cycling performance. Data were collected from competitive cyclists using a custom wireless sensor network that captured heart rate, body temperature, pedaling power, and cadence during indoor and outdoor velodrome sessions. An LSTM model designed to handle sequential dependencies is benchmarked against MLP and ANFIS predictors. Experimental results show that LSTM consistently achieves the lowest errors for multi-step-ahead speed and finishing time predictions, with improved generalization relative to MLP and ANFIS. These findings highlight the advantages of deep learning for modeling the dynamic, nonlinear nature of cycling performance and demonstrate the potential of LSTM-based predictors for real-time athlete monitoring, training optimization, and strategic decision-making in track cycling. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
