TI  - Accuracy of the Playermaker 2.0 IMU in Tracking Running Distance at Varying Speeds in Elite Youth Soccer
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Symposium on  Inertial Sensors and Systems, ISISS
DO  - 10.1109/ISA67150.2025.11279281
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032642389?origin=resultslist
AB  - Nowadays, inertial measurement units (IMUs) have become established for determining training and match loads in several sports, mainly based on GNSS technology. Their ability to measure external load parameters such as running speed has been extensively studied. But it has also been shown, that that those systems often provide inaccurate data estimates, due to unsuitable sensors or algorithms. Research regarding the accuracy of IMU-alone Systems is rare. Accordingly, the aim of this study was to evaluate the accuracy of a foot-mounted IMU system, which is based on the MPU-9150 inertial sensor (InvenSense, USA), in measuring total running distance, distance covered within specific speed zones, and maximum sprint velocity in comparison to a 3D motion capture system (Qualisys, 100 Hz). The statistical analysis includes descriptive statistics, Intraclass Correlation Coefficients (ICC), Bland-Altman agreement analysis, paired-sample t-tests, and Cohen's d effect size calculations to quantify systematic differences between systems. The results indicate an increasing deviation between the two systems as running speed increases. While "good" to "excellent" agreement was found at low to moderate speed (< 4 m/s) (ICC: 0.850 - 0.962), higher speeds (> 5.5 m/s) showed moderate agreement (ICC: 0.717) with greater scatter in the LoA (-28 m to -2.99 m). A significant difference was found between the two systems in the categories "> 5.5 m/s" and "Vmax". These findings align with previous research suggesting that cumulative signal errors ("drift") arising from inadequate filtering algorithms are responsible for reduced IMU accuracy at higher speeds or complex movement patterns such as rapid changes of direction or varying stride lengths during sprints. ©2025 IEEE.
