TI  - Design of an Energy-Efficient Vlsi-Based Ecg Signal Processing System for Real-Time Health Monitoring
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 5th MEC International Conference on Advanced Data Analytics and Artificial Intelligence for Sustainable Smart Cities, ICADAAI 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICADAAI64945.2026.11679266
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052641543?origin=resultslist
AB  - This paper provides continuous real- time monitoring of patient' s health status a system has been developed based on energy-efficient VLSI technology to process ECG signals. The solution incorporates an analog front end with very low noise as well as a low-power ADC designed to be aware of power consumption and scalable digital processing cores specifically for arrhythmia detection and heart rate variability analysis. Specialized processes have been created for processing of ECG data using hardware- efficient algorithm implementations such as: Adaptive Filtering, QRS Detection and Feature Extraction on multiple parallel low-power processing units and the use of approximate computational techniques along with clock gating mechanisms to minimize dynamic power dissipation. Hierarchical on-chip memory and a lightweight data compression technique decreases the energy consumed for data movement/ storage. Dynamic voltage scaling (DVS) and duty cycling (D/C) are used for running time power management based on signal activity. Design tradeoffs between performance/power, silicon area, and diagnostic accuracy have been evaluated through cycle- accurate simulation and use of Field Programmable Gate Arrays. Results demonstrate significant energy savings while achieving clinically acceptable ECG fidelity/ detection performance for wearable/implantable devices. © 2026 IEEE.
