DO  - 10.1088/1742-6596/3113/1/012007
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019302801?origin=resultslist
AB  - X80 pipeline steel is widely used in long-distance oil and gas transportation due to its high strength and toughness. However, reducing wall thickness to cut costs can lead to stress concentration during forming and under external loads. Since stress can significantly alter the magnetic properties of ferromagnetic materials, magnetic characterization techniques provide a promising approach for stress evaluation. Using X80 pipeline steel as the research subject, this study integrates theoretical analysis with experimental investigations to establish a magneto-mechanical coupling equation derived from energy minimization principles. The research elucidates the quantitative relationship between coercivity and applied stress, while systematically characterizing the coercivity-stress response patterns during both elastic and plastic deformation stages.  © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.
