AB  - Heart rate variability (HRV) is a powerful non-invasive indicator for assessing autonomic nervous system function and the level of physiological load imposed on the body. Despite substantial progress in applying HRV within sports medicine and cardiac monitoring, the automatic classification of physical activity types based solely on HRV remains insufficiently explored. In this study, we investigate the feasibility of classifying three levels of physical activity (sitting, walking, running) using 67 HRV features derived from standard ECG signal processing. A high-quality public dataset comprising synchronized ECG and PPG recordings from 22 healthy volunteers performing different activities was utilized. After extracting diagnostically meaningful HRV metrics, feature selection was conducted, followed by a comparison of six machine learning models using Leave-One-Subject-Out validation. The results demonstrate that HRV features can effectively discriminate between physical activity levels, with ensemble-based classifiers achieving the highest performance (...78% macro-F1). The proposed approach may serve as a basis for developing human state monitoring algorithms in wearable sensor systems. © 2026 IEEE.
