TI  - Non-intrusive power load monitoring based on multi-scale convolution and layer-wise residual graph convolution
PY  - 2025
T2  - Measurement: Journal of the International Measurement Confederation
VL  - 246
C7  - 116593
DO  - 10.1016/j.measurement.2024.116593
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85214333967?origin=resultslist
AB  - The existing non-intrusive load monitoring algorithms lack the capability to capture long-distance dependencies within the input sequences, which leads to low accuracy in appliance state recognition and significant errors in power disaggregation. To address these issues, research on a load decomposition algorithm based on graph representation learning has been conducted. Within the framework of graph representation learning, optimizations are conducted on two aspects: graph data construction and graph convolutional structures. A load decomposition method based on multi-scale convolution and hierarchical residual graph convolution is proposed. Utilizing multi-scale convolution to extract multi-dimensional power features and constructing a graph node feature matrix, the method further assigns different weights to the connecting edges based on temporal correlations and feature relevance. This enhances the graph data's capability to represent time series. Building on the residual graph convolution, the method utilizes parallel adaptive adjacency matrices to extract multi-angle differentiated data features, thereby preventing the phenomenon of graph node over-smoothing that can occur as the depth of graph convolution operations increases. The hybrid attention mechanism is combined with summation pooling to alleviate the problem of information loss during pooling operation. The model is validated by the AMPds2 dataset, and the results show that the model in this paper reduces the average absolute error mean by 50.40% and improves the power decomposition accuracy by 8.95% compared with the single graph convolution model. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd
