TI  - Emotion-Aware Workflows: Utilizing Wearable Biosensors and Edge AI to Detect Stress Patterns and Optimize Employee Well-being in Real Time
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Communication, Computing and Emerging Technologies, IC3ET 2026
SP  - 481
EP  - 486
DO  - 10.1109/IC3ET64989.2026.11467251
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037454914?origin=resultslist
AB  - Work-related stress is a significant issue in today's workplaces, with effects on productivity, health, and retention. Conventional methods of stress monitoring, such as surveys or audits, are lagging indicators and cannot provide real-time insights into variations in conditions. In this work, we present an emotion-aware workflow framework that combines wearable biosensors with edge AI technology to identify stress patterns and provide in-the-moment interventions. Multimodal physiological data (i.e., HRV, GSR, and EEG-lite signals) were recorded from sixty employees during four weeks. A CNNLSTM model running on an edge gateway achieved 92 % classification accuracy with a latency of less than 250 ms. Workflow interventions, including micro-break and task redistribution alerts, led to significant reductions in bouts of stress and helped balance the workload. The work broadens the scope of affective computing to encompass organizational processes, highlighting the possibility of privacy-preserving and ubiquitous, real-time well-being systems that improve both employee health and workplace productivity. © 2026 IEEE.
