DO  - 10.1061/9780784486993.072
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049192843?origin=resultslist
AB  - Construction workers frequently experience high cognitive load due to physically demanding tasks, hazardous environments, and continuous decision making. These factors can reduce productivity, accelerate fatigue, and increase the risk of accidents. Existing approaches to assessing cognitive load in construction primarily rely on self-reported surveys such as the NASA TLX, which are often intrusive, prone to bias, and incapable of capturing real-time fluctuations. While electrodermal activity (EDA) has shown strong potential as a wearable marker of cognitive load, most systems remain limited to signal acquisition and lack autonomous real-time assessment in the field. To address this gap, this paper presents an Arduino-based wearable module that integrates flexible EDA sensing with an edge AI algorithm for continuous, low-latency cognitive load monitoring. Experimental evaluation shows that the embedded AI model running on a personal smartphone achieves an average accuracy of over 81% in detecting cognitive load variations, with an inference latency of approximately 98 milliseconds, enabling real-time monitoring during high-risk tasks. This work demonstrates the feasibility of real-time, edge-based cognitive load monitoring in construction and lays the foundation for scalable wearable systems for proactive stress management and improved worker safety. © 2026 ASCE.
