AB  - Human Activity Recognition (HAR) is a rapidly developing research subject with applications in healthcare, security monitoring, and human-computer interaction. It allows for continuous monitoring, behavior analysis, and the creation of intelligent context-aware systems. HAR can be done by wireless technologies like Wi-Fi or radar signals to infer movements without direct contact, wearable sensors like accelerometers and gyroscopes to record motion data, cameras and computer vision to analyze body movement, gestures, or postures, and audio and acoustic sensing to identify activity-related sounds. Out of which wearable devices such as Polar H10 could be a potential solution for reliable activity monitoring which is the focus of this study. The proposed study is an attempt to utilize a new dataset (PPG GALAXY) for ECG signals and perform a comparative analysis of machine learning models for Human Activity Recognition. For this study, four physical activities taken standing, walking, jogging, and running were classified using seventeen extracted features, including statistical, entropy-based, and heart rate variability (HRV) measures.It employs four ML models Random Forest, Bagging, Boosting, and Decision Tree for comparative analysis of HAR performance with evaluation carried out using 10-fold cross validation. Among these, Random Forest achieved the highest performance, with an accuracy of 8 0. 5 6 % and an F1-score of 8 1. 0 0 %, demonstrating robustness to motion artifacts. © 2025 IEEE.
