AB  - This chapter explores the physiological foundations essential to the human weapon system (HWS), focusing on energy generation through the phosphagen pathway, glycolysis, and oxidative phosphorylation, alongside the neuromuscular processes driving motor unit recruitment and skeletal muscle function. It highlights the performance model of conditioning, emphasizing oxygen consumption, lactate threshold, and movement economy as critical determinants of endurance and efficiency in demanding operational environments. By examining muscle fiber composition, energy metabolism, and training adaptations, the chapter provides a framework for optimizing strength and conditioning programs tailored to the unique demands of HWS professionals, ultimately enhancing their resilience and operational effectiveness. © 2025 The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
