TI  - PERIODIZATION: Planning Fitness Training in Football
PY  - 2025
T2  - Strength and Conditioning for Football: From Science to Practice
SP  - 74
EP  - 88
DO  - 10.4324/9781003383475-6
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105018252804?origin=resultslist
AB  - In consideration of the multi-component performance dimension and long seasons with frequent competitive games, as well as the long recovery after matches, planning of training in elite football needs to balance several issues. First, well-defined training categories need to be established within the different performance areas (ranging from speed and peak power to high-intensity intermittent exercise capacity, in addition to fatigue resistance allowing these intense actions to be repeated throughout the game). Secondly, recovery from matches and the different training types needs to be taken into consideration and organized in individual sessions during the weekly microcycles. The organization of individual sessions may lower the risk of overuse injuries and increase players’ preparation and performance in the next match. In the first part of the preseason period, physiological variables associated with durability/fatigue resilience and basic muscle strength need to be maintained, and thereafter, a multifaceted rebuilding period should be initiated ensuring that both aerobic, anaerobic, and strength variables are optimally upregulated. This can be attained by a combination of small-sided game (SSG) training and dedicated physical training targeting relevant physiological parameters. During the season these parameters must be maintained (or further developed if possible) by having a continuous high focus on aerobic high-intensity training, speed endurance training, speed training, and strength training. Special emphasis must be placed on loading regular and substitute players differently and taking care of individual game demands. Moreover, planning of the training load must be prioritized according to different game schedules and types of tournament. Finally, reliable and valid football-specific testing must be applied on a regular basis and used as guidance in the planning, both at the team and individual level. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Marco Beato, Chris Bishop and Anthony Turner.
