DO  - 10.13238/j.issn.1004-2954.202212240005
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85213213523?origin=resultslist
AB  - Aiming at the limitations of traditional train comfort research in the long-term service monitoring of subway train comfort, this paper presents a comprehensive evaluation method of subway train comfort based on mobile terminals. By investigating the types of sensors integrated in mobile terminals, it is determined that the factors affecting the comfort of subway trains are vibration, noise and illuminance. Combined with questionnaire survey and analytic hierarchy process, the weight coefficients of each factor's influence on the comfort of subway trains are determined. The evaluation models and evaluation standards of each factor are established based on the existing standards and simulation experiments, and then the evaluation method of subway train comfort is determined. The proposed method is integrated into the self-developed mobile terminal APP to realize large-scale collection of train comfort data. In order to objectively verify the feasibility of this method, the experimental data from professional measuring instruments, mobile terminals and wearable devices are collected in the subway line experiment. The results of the analysis show that: when the train is running smoothly, the relative errors of the vibration, noise and illuminance comfort evaluation values collected by the mobile terminal shall not exceed 5%, 15% and 18% respectively; the noise comfort evaluation value accounts for 67. 8% and 76. 5% of the total evaluation value under two working conditions, therefore the greatest influence on train comfort is noise; the evaluation results of subway train comfort based on the proposed method tend to be consistent with the analysis results of heart rate variability frequency domain indicators (LF, HF and LF/HF), which objectively verify the feasibility of the evaluation method. The mobile terminal-driven subway train comfort evaluation method can provide new ways and ideas for large-scale monitoring of subway train comfort and long-term service performance evolution analysis. © 2024 Editorial Office of Railway Standard Design. All rights reserved.
