AB  - This study evaluates the effects of disclosing drivers' physiological and operational data on passenger trust and driver workload in bus services. Surveys were conducted for longdistance and on-demand buses in Japan and Malaysia, alongside electrocardiogram (ECG) measurements of professional drivers. Passengers consistently preferred behavioral indicators - such as driving history and heart-rate variability (HRV) - which significantly improved perceived security and trust. HRV analysis confirmed lower SDNN values during driving, indicating increased psychophysiological load. Cross-country comparisons revealed that Japanese drivers experienced stress and reluctance toward disclosure, whereas Malaysian drivers showed acceptance. These results demonstrate that real-time visualization of driver state can enhance transparency and safety assurance but must be implemented with cultural sensitivity and respect for driver autonomy. © 2025 IEEE.
