AB  - Stress fractures pose a significant risk to individuals in physically demanding professions such as military personnel, first responders and elite athletes. We propose an innovative multimodal system that pairs chest-worn Movesense Flash wearable to measure inertial variables and heart rate variability (HRV) with synchronized FLIR RGB cameras for markerless motion analysis during four selected exercises: a 1 km running, single-hop test, push-ups, and a 4 × 10 m shuttle run. The measurements are complemented by subjective assessment of health and fatigue through validated questionnaires. By aggregating biomechanical variables, autonomic-load indicators, and subjective health data, our framework aims to enable early identification of individuals at elevated risk for stress fractures. This multimodal system can be used for early intervention, injury risk reduction, and optimization of training and education programs in occupations with high physical demands.  © 2025 IEEE.
