VL  - 57
C7  - 103189
DO  - 10.1016/j.tsep.2024.103189
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85214281633?origin=resultslist
AB  - Basketball, as a high-intensity, high-antagonistic sport, puts forward extremely high demands on athletes’ physical fitness and endurance. Muscle heat energy consumption is a key index to measure the physical condition of athletes. In this study, a dynamic image simulation system of heat radiation was developed by using light sensor technology to monitor muscle heat energy consumption in basketball game simulation. The study selected basketball players as the research objects, designed a series of simulated game scenes, including different intensity of offensive and defensive transition, fast running and jumping. The thermal radiation images of athletes in simulated competitions are captured in real time by using high-precision light sensors. These light sensors are able to pick up tiny changes in thermal radiation generated by muscle activity and convert them into image data. The collected thermal radiation images were dynamically simulated to generate dynamic image sequence of muscle thermal energy consumption, and the image sequence was analyzed to extract key parameters of muscle thermal energy consumption. The thermal radiation dynamic image simulation system was compared with the traditional monitoring methods (such as heart rate monitoring, subjective fatigue score, etc.), and the thermal radiation dynamic image simulation system was built, and the system was applied in the basketball game simulation to monitor the athlete's muscle heat energy consumption. The results show that the system can accurately capture the muscle activity and heat energy consumption of athletes in the competition, and the dynamic image sequence generated clearly shows the spatial and temporal distribution characteristics of muscle heat energy consumption. Compared with traditional monitoring methods, the thermal radiation dynamic image simulation system has obvious advantages in real-time and accuracy of data. Especially in high-intensity competition scenarios, the system can provide more detailed and accurate information about muscle thermal energy consumption, which helps coaches and athletes better understand the physical changes in competition. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
