TI  - Motion State Monitor Based on Acceleration Sensor
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 7th International Conference on Electronic Engineering and Informatics, EEI 2025
SP  - 305
EP  - 311
DO  - 10.1109/EEI67650.2025.11334813
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033235740?origin=resultslist
AB  - With the rapid advancement of wearable devices and mobile health monitoring technologies, motion state recognition based on accelerometers has emerged as a research hotspot. To enhance the accuracy and real-time performance of human motion recognition, this study employs a triaxial accelerometer to continuously collect and analyze five typical motion states: stationary, walking, running, stair climbing/descending, and jumping. Data acquisition lasted 60 seconds, with signals processed through a fourth-order 10 Hz Butterworth low-pass filter to suppress high-frequency noise. Key features - including mean, standard deviation, root mean square (RMS), peak-to-peak amplitude, energy, and inter-axis correlation - were extracted via combined acceleration calculation, sliding window feature extraction, and visualization analysis. These features quantified differences between movement patterns across time domain, frequency domain, and statistical domain perspectives. Results revealed characteristic frequencies of approximately 3 Hz for walking and 6 Hz for running, with jumping exhibiting distinct pulse peaks. Stair climbing/descending demonstrated characteristic vertical oscillations. Furthermore, significant differences in Z-axis standard deviation and peak total acceleration provided clear boundaries for feature differentiation. A preliminary model was constructed using multi-feature fusion and supervised classification methods, achieving an overall average recognition accuracy of 88%. The recognition rates for stationary and jumping states reached 100% and 95%, respectively. The confusion matrix revealed some misclassification between running and stair climbing, suggesting that future improvements could incorporate frequency-domain enhanced features and multi-modal signal fusion to further optimize classification performance. This study provides experimental evidence and a feature framework for human motion recognition based on accelerometers, offering technical reference for the implementation of smart wearable devices, rehabilitation training monitoring, and behavior perception systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
