UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85196904660?origin=resultslist
AB  - BACKGROUND: Adaptation to short-term overload training may differ from inter-individual variation during a given exercise. This study aimed to elucidate the relationship between relative changes in aerobic power and training load in 2-week taper training following a 2-week overload training. METHODS: Seventeen healthy men participated in a 5-week training program consisting of pedaling exercises and were assigned a control (CON, N.=7) and an experimental group (EXP, N.=10). Following a 1-week common training program as familiarization in both groups, the EXP conducted 2-week overload training followed by 2-week taper training. The CON continued to do the same training program as familiarization period for the later 4 weeks. To determine aerobic power, an incrementally-loaded and a 60 s Wingate test were measured at baseline and once a week. External and internal loads were derived from an ergometer’s load and heart rate. We computed monotony and strain in training sessions using both loads. RESULTS: For EXP, the relationships between strain derived from the internal load and the relative change in maximal aerobic power during overload training were moderate to large, but the corresponding relationships were unclear in CON. CONCLUSIONS: The current findings indicate that the individual’s variation in aerobic adaptation to the 2-week overload training may be partly due to the individual difference in strain derived from heart rate during training sessions. © 2023 EDIZIONI MINERVA MEDICA.
