SP  - 1018
EP  - 1026
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85205002318?origin=resultslist
AB  - Many people perform physically demanding activities daily. Farmers experience high physical load in several tasks at the farm. Wearable technology gets stronger in monitoring physiological variables and becomes cheaper. Many problems and health issues are related to the energy balance in body and mind. We have developed algorithms to monitor continuously the metabolic energy used for physical tasks. The technology, developed first for humans, also applies to monitor the physical energy use of animals. Ten young amateur Flemish cyclists competing actively cycled on a race bike connected to a roller in the lab under thermoneutral conditions. Different gear combinations at different powers were applied in slots of 5 minutes, for 90 minutes. They were questioned about their comfort and subjective fatigue, using the Borg scale and the subjective rate of perceived exertion, respectively, at every power/gear change. Throughout the experiment, per second, the power exerted was gathered via a bike roller, and the participant's heart rate and movement were collected via wearables. The algorithm first decomposes the heart rate into different components: the basal, physical, thermal, and mental components. In this work, we can demonstrate that the results of this continuous monitoring tool agree on average in 70 to 80 % with the answers collected from questionnaires about the subjective perceived rate of exertion and comfort. The technology is available to be used for farmers while its application to livestock requires a user-friendly sensor that does not need recharging. © 2024 11th European Conference on Precision Livestock Farming. All rights reserved.
