UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85169366348?origin=resultslist
AB  - Bone stress injury (BSI) is an overuse injury seen in young athletes and exists on a continuum of bone edema to fracture line. Youth athletes participating in running and jumping sports are at elevated risk. The tibia, fibula, and metatarsals are the most common locations. Prior fracture, higher training loads, as well as anatomic, biomechanical, and biological factors (i.e., low energy availability and menstrual dysfunction) increase the risk for BSI. Clinical examination includes the presence of bone pain and may be confirmed using imaging including X-ray and magnetic resonance imaging. Treatment involves immobilization and reduction of weight-bearing, cross training with graduated return to play, optimizing nutrition and sleep, and addressing comorbidities including low bone mineral density, eating disorder or disordered eating, and menstrual dysfunction. Prevention of BSI includes ensuring adequate nutrition (including caloric intake, calcium, and vitamin D), youth sports sampling, and avoiding training errors. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
