TI  - Very weak relationships between pre-match well-being scores and high-intensity locomotor activities in professional soccer players
PY  - 2023
T2  - The Culture of Team Sports
SP  - 27
EP  - 44
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85183085892?origin=resultslist
AB  - Some studies suggest that a reduction in subjective well-being has a negative effect on the subsequent external load during soccer training sessions. However, it remains questionable whether soccer players regulate their running and movement intensity during match-play as they do in training in response to changes in subjective well-being. Thus, the aim of this study was to analyze the relationships between pre-match well-being scores and high-intensity locomotor activities during official matches in professional soccer players. Seventeen outfield male professional players of a soccer team participated in this study. Data was collected over ten official matches during the 2022-2023 season, each one separated by one week. A five-point Likert questionnaire (scores 1-5) was completed 90 minutes before each match. Each player was asked to respond subjectively about pre-match perceived fatigue, sleep quality, delayed-onset muscle soreness and stress. The following metrics were recorded during soccer matches: total distance at high-intensity running (21.0-24.0 km·h-1), total distance at sprinting (>24 km·h-1), number of high-intensity runs (>21.0 km·h-1), number of sprints (>24.0 km·h-1), maximum speed, number of very high-intensity accelerations (>4 m·s-2), number of high-intensity accelerations (>3 m·s-2), number of highintensity decelerations (<-3 m·s-2), and number of very high-intensity decelerations (<-4 m·s-2). The players' delayed-onset muscle soreness had a significant influence on total distance at sprinting during matches, but the proportion of the variance explained by delayed-onset muscle soreness indicated a very weak effect (R2 = 0.037, p = 0.034). Perceived fatigue, delayed-onset muscle soreness and stress had a significant influence on maximum speed, but the proportion of the variance also indicated a very weak effect (R2 = 0.112, p = 0.003). Sleep and fatigue had a very weak effect on the number of very high-intensity accelerations and high-intensity decelerations (R2 = 0.093, p < 0.001; R2 = 0.048, p = 0.015; respectively), and fatigue and delayed-onset muscle soreness had a very weak effect on the number of very high-intensity decelerations (R2 = 0.106, p = 0.001). These findings suggest that sprinting performance and the number of high-intensity accelerations and decelerations of professional soccer players could be slightly regulated during official matches in proportion to the perceived pre-match well-being. © 2024 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
