TI  - How wearable devices can assist with Crewmember's mental health management
PY  - 2023
T2  - Maritime Wellbeing
SP  - 61
EP  - 71
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85172680391?origin=resultslist
AB  - During the COVID-19 pandemic, crewmembers' smooth operation and management were disturbed by the restriction measures imposed worldwide. Lockdowns and traveling bans caused major delays in crew changes. Under these circumstances, crew members exhibited several cases of intense behaviors as they were mentally and physically exhausted. The chapter deals with the psychological health of crew members and the means that can be used to avoid the psycho impact of incidents or stressful situations like the one previously mentioned. The use of wearable technologies can assist in this direction. Through qualitative research, the chapter aims to provide crew managers with methods through which wearable devices can assist in managing crewmembers' mental health. The wearable devices can measure the heart rate variability, the electrodermal activity, and the skin body temperature of the crew, which can show anxiety levels, stress, mood, and an individual's mental health. Smart tools can take many forms, from headbands to glasses or watches. Those devices are designed to be comfortable enough for any crew member during work or throughout the day. After the smart gadgets' diagnosis, a professional psychologist is crucial to confirm and examine the crewmember's mental health, thus his well-being. By collecting those data and running them through an evaluation process algorithm, crew managers can assess the individual and the overall mental health of the crew members. The chapter reviews the related literature dealing with seafarer's mental health and contributes by examining how wearable devices can assist in assisting seafarers in need. © 2023 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
