TI  - Cognitive, Sleep, and Autonomic Responses to Induction of a Ketogenic Diet in Military Personnel: A Pilot Study
PY  - 2022
T2  - Aerospace Medicine and Human Performance
VL  - 93
IS  - 6
SP  - 507
EP  - 516
DO  - 10.3357/AMHP.6015.2022
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85132265507?origin=resultslist
AB  - BACKGROUND: T his pilot study examined the effect of a 2-wk ketogenic diet (KD) compared with a carbohydrate (CHO) diet in military personnel on cognitive performance, mood, sleep, and heart rate variability (HRV). METHODS: A randomized-controlled, cross-over trial was conducted with eight male military personnel (age, 36 ±6 7 yr; body mass, 83.7 ± 9.2 kg; BMI, 26.0 ± 2.3 kg. m-2). Subjects ingested their habitual diet for 7 d (baseline), then an iso-energetic KD (~25 g CHO/d) or CHO diet (~285 g CHO/d) for 14 d (adaptation), separated by a 12-d washout. HRV, fasting capillary blood D-βHB, and glucose concentration, mood, and sleep were measured daily. Cognitive performance was measured on the 7th day of baseline and the 7th and 14th days of adaptation. Data were analyzed using a series of linear mixed models. RESULTS: Mean weekly D-βHB was higher (95% CI, 10.34 to 12.38 mmol · L21) and glucose was lower (20.45 to 20.21 mmol · L-1)(20.45 to 20.21 mmol · L-1) in the KD compared with the CHO diet. Cognitive performance (Psychomotor Vigilance Task, 2-choice reaction time, and running memory continuous performance test) and mean weekly fatigue, vigor, and sleep (sleep duration, sleep efficiency, and sleep onset latency) were similar between diets. A diet 3 week interaction for HRV approached significance, with exploratory analyses suggesting HRV was lower compared with baseline during week-2 adapt (227 to 14 ms) in the KD. DISCUSSION: A 2-wk KD induction in military personnel does not appear to affect cognitive performance, mood, or sleep, but may lower HRV, indicating increased physiological Stress. © by the Aerospace Medical Association, Alexandria, VA.
