TI  - Effects of train-induced vibration on precision instrument in a far-field building
PY  - 2021
T2  - "Advances in Acoustics, Noise and Vibration - 2021" Proceedings of the 27th International Congress on Sound and Vibration, ICSV 2021
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85117489280?origin=resultslist
AB  - Among all the recent improvements of underground railways in metropolitan cities, ground-borne vibrations caused by underground trains remain an extraordinary showstopper in the environmental and transportation communities. In some particular cases, tiny vibrations can disturb the routine use of the precision instrument. The environmental vibration problems caused by underground trains involve many factors, including train operating parameters, track structure, soil properties, building structure, etc., which make it especially challenging to solve such an extensive dynamic system efficiently and accurately. This paper presents a numerical study of train-induced micro-vibrations in a far-field building. A three-step time-domain prediction method for train-induced vibrations of a large-scale structure is proposed in this work. In the first step, a spatial vehicle-track coupled dynamic model is established in the time domain to obtain the rail vertical and lateral rail supporting forces (fastening forces). In the second step, the fastener force is used as the external load of the track-tunnel-soil finite element (FE) model, and the vibration response of the soil at the site location of the far-field building is solved. The third step is to use the vibration response of the soil as an external excitation and apply it to the FE model of the building in a consistent manner to acquire the vibration response. On this basis, underground train-induced micro-vibrations at far-field building floors are predicted, and the service safety of the precision instrument is evaluated according to its vibration requirements. The calculation results show that in this project, the far-field building vibration caused by the train running at the highest operating speed under full load conditions meets the instrument's requirements for transient and steady-state vibration, and will not affect the regular use of the equipment. © "Advances in Acoustics, Noise and Vibration - 2021" Proceedings of the 27th International Congress on Sound and Vibration, ICSV 2021. All rights reserved.
