AB  - Previous studies have shown that time-dependent cardiorespiratory responses before and after the initiation of dynamic exercise at various intensities depend on the relationship between exercise training and its exercise intensity. However, it is not clear when and to what extent such differences in training intensity for the development of long-term physiological adaptation affect cardiorespiratory function during exercise. The subjects were 16 college athletes who were divided into 95% and 80% intensity training groups (TG). After high-intensity interval training (HIT), peak values of oxygen uptake and minute ventilation were significantly increased in both groups. At 6 weeks after the start of HIT, the rate of increase in heart rate (HR) immediately after the start of step exercise and the peak HR significantly increased only at 95%TG, with no significant changes observed at 80% TG. In conclusion, the long-term physiological adaptation by exercise training in the time-dependent cardiorespiratory response before and after the start of exercise depended on the intensity of exercise training and appeared mainly in the autonomic cardiac control system after 6 weeks. © 2021, Japan Soc. of Med. Electronics and Biol. Engineering. All rights reserved.
