UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85150947068?origin=resultslist
AB  - Athletics includes different sport events carrying specific risks of injury according to the specific activity. Sprint, hurdles, and jumps have a high incidence of acute traumatic lesions, while middle- and long-distance running mainly predispose to overuse injuries. Biomechanical stresses, training workloads, and duration of practice play an important role. Tendinopathies, muscle strains, and stress fractures are common. The relationship between rate of injuries and training loads seems to be directly proportional. Young athletes have been reported to eat higher injury risks than adults, especially in middle- and long-distance running. This particularly concerns the adolescent athletes. The musculoskeletal system of growing athlete is more vulnerable to specific injuries, such as epiphyseal traumatic injuries or apophysis overuse injuries. A higher rate of injuries occurs during training than during competition (60-91%). Sprint and hurdles show a higher risk of hamstring and rectus femoris strains, as well as Achilles tendon ruptures. Long-distance events are more prone to anterior knee pain, medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles tendinopathy, iliotibial band friction syndrome, plantar fasciitis, and stress fractures. Anterior knee pain and patellar tendinopathy frequently affect jumpers. Pole vault can be considered as the highest risk jump event, being the track and field discipline with the highest risk of mortality, mainly due to the landing phase directly onto the head or neck. Proper athlete selection and training in an appropriate environment are the best prevention strategies, and a prompt and accurate management of acute or overuse injuries is the best way to safely resume competition. © ISAKOS 2021.
