DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-323-67949-7.00003-3
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85094616389?origin=resultslist
AB  - Runners with injuries can benefit from a comprehensive evaluation that consists of a complete review of medical and training history, physical function assessments, pain symptoms, shoewear, and biomechanics while running to find characteristics that are related to injury. Sudden changes to training load (distance, speed, or frequency of runs), training surface, running gear, shoe type or orthotics, foot strike, or running form may trigger pain and injury onset. Functional assessments should include load-bearing tests (single-leg squats, balance) and non-load-bearing tests (flexibility of ankle, hip, and toes) to determine potential links in the kinetic chain that may not be able to transfer or sustain forces during the stance phase of the gait cycle. Physician involvement is recommended if the runner is symptomatic, and physical therapy consults are important for correcting biomechanical issues found in biomechanical analyses. © 2020 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
