T2  - The Science of Swimming and Aquatic Activities
SP  - 171
EP  - 183
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047760935?origin=resultslist
AB  - Swimming is an increasingly popular sport in the world. One of the most important objectives of the swimmer’s multidisciplinary team is to improve performance, avoid overtraining and achieve high-performance peaks with low fatigability. It is known that fatigue and overtraining alter the mechanics of the swimmers and decrease the motion cadence. Specifically, there is an inversely proportional relationship between upper limbs length and number of cycles with fatigue. It is well known that there is an inverse proportional relationship between the numbers of cycles of upper limbs with fatigue, the greater fatigue and the slower the motion cycle. Associated with the above, the torque generated by an optimal and efficient motion cycle achieves less upper limb movements per meter; therefore fatigue directly affects the swimmer’s competition times, both in the number of motion and the torque generated in each one of them. There is evidence to support the use of active recovery exercises in swimming, which decrease serological levels of lactate faster and homogeneously after a continuous training load if compared to passive rest, having a significant impact on improving performance. Lactate levels indirectly reflect muscle metabolism, but does not produce fatigue. The objective of this chapter is to review both active and passive recuperation strategies in swimming, the effect on fatigue and how they affect performance and finally to describe and suggest specific guidelines to apply. © 2018 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
