TI  - The problem of arrhythmias in endurance athletes: Are heart rate monitors valuable tools for diagnosing arrhythmias?
PY  - 2018
T2  - Horizons in World Cardiovascular Research
VL  - 15
SP  - 1
EP  - 64
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85058921849?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cardiac arrhythmias are not uncommon in apparently healthy men and women. Based on recent epidemiological studies, in 4-10% of the adult population, various forms of arrhythmias could be detected, most frequently atrial fibrillation (AF) and ventricular extrasystoles. These arrhythmias are more common in men than in women and the frequency increased with ageing. In most young individuals without structural heart disease, short runs of atrial tachycardia, ventricular tachycardia or atrial fibrillation do not require treatment. Exercise-related palpitations, vertigo and syncope may be caused by benign etiologies but may also herald lifethreatening arrhythmias. Arrhythmias have been observed and described also in various groups of endurance athletes, even those with excellent physical capacity and considerable sports achievements. Endurance training does not cause increased mortality but can trigger a lethal arrhythmia, which reveals itself more frequently in athletes with heart disease than in non-training patients with heart disease. For this reason, arrhythmia and the associated increased risk of sudden death in athletes, particularly in young people compared to their inactive peers, are reasons for developing eligibility guidelines for sports and for developing procedural rules in the event that irregularities are detected during athlete monitoring tests. Certain rhythm disturbances, such as paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (AF) or paroxysmal atrial flutter, are more common in endurance athletes compared to inactive ones. Finally, the suspicion of arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia (ARVC), induced by long-term exercise among athletes of endurance sports is still a challenge for further research. In recent years, we observed the extensive increase of cardiac patients performing physical activities, particularly running and cycling, as primary and secondary methods for preventing cardiovascular diseases, including coronary heart disease and hypertension. Physically active patients with known cardiac diseases, often running long-distance racing or marathons, are particularly exposed to ventricular and supraventricular arrhythmias. Therefore, the precise diagnosis of these findings is essential and potentially lifesaving but is often a challenge for sports physicians and cardiologists. Millions of physically active individuals worldwide use heart rate monitors (HRMs) to control their exercise intensity. In many cases, the HRM indicates an unusually high heart rate (HR) or even arrhythmias during training. Unfortunately, studies assessing the reliability of these devices to help control HR disturbances during exercise did not exist until recently. Are heart rate monitors valuable tools for diagnosing arrhythmias in endurance athletes? In this chapter the authors present and discuss various aspects of arrhythmias in endurance competitive and leisure-time sportsmen including master athletes based on their extensive experience and international literature. The authors also discuss the value of HRM for evaluating exercise intensity and for diagnosis of "arrhythmia" based on our own recently published study. A standard procedure for dealing with suspected "arrhythmia," useful both for athletes, their coaches or doctors based on HRM indications is proposed. © 2018 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
