TI  - A USEFUL PROTOCOL FOR LOW TO SEVERE SWIMMING INTENSITIES: PHYSIOLOGICAL AND BIOMECHANICAL CHARACTERIZATION
PY  - 2018
T2  - The Science of Swimming and Aquatic Activities
SP  - 227
EP  - 243
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047763212?origin=resultslist
AB  - Swimming is a cyclic and continuous sport in which both physiological and biomechanical factors are major performance-determining factors. For more than 80 years, swimming together with running and cycling, have been, one of the primary focuses of research in Sport Sciences. Over the years, numerous test methodologies have been applied. The general aim of this chapter is to identify and characterize the best variant of a front crawl incremental protocol frequently used for assessing physiological and biomechanical variables along a wide range of swimming intensities. Evaluation and control of swimming training has become an accepted and essential task of a coach. Obtaining quantitative data on the swimmer’s performance status and adequacy of training is decisive to a more objective and precise training process. Beginning in the 1980s, swimmers have engaged in strenuous high volume training programs, swimming multiple times the distance they perform in competition. Swimmers typically train 10 to 15 km per day while competing for the most part in 50, 100 and 200 m events. In a typical day, they perform approximately 100 times more volume than their competitive distances. This is a training philosophy that has been passed on from generation to generation and with success. It is nevertheless rather intriguing since it seems contradictory that swimming at slow pace for thousands of meters will make a swimmer faster in events lasting only a few minutes. To challenge this paradigm and introduce more objective and specific training protocols, it is fundamental to have well established tests to clearly define training intensity targets. By reviewing both the continuous and intermittent incremental protocols available in the literature, as well as their variants with diverse step lengths, and using both physiological and biomechanical variables, we want to examine why the 7 x 200 m (30 s interval) intermittent incremental protocol has been the most frequently selected for assessing swimmers training status. We will focus our attention on oxygen uptake, blood lactate concentrations, heart rate, general biomechanical parameters, segmental kinematics, inter-limb coordination, intracyclic velocity variations and propelling efficiency. The anaerobic threshold and the maximal oxygen uptake will be used as well stablished intensity markers to clarify how this protocol can be helpful in defining low to moderate, heavy, severe and extreme swimming intensity levels. We will finally move on to discuss more valid and reliable training zones. © 2018 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
