TI  - BIOCHEMICAL MONITORING OF SWIMMING TRAINING: OLD AND NEW CONCEPTS
PY  - 2018
T2  - The Science of Swimming and Aquatic Activities
SP  - 79
EP  - 93
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047760020?origin=resultslist
AB  - Biochemical monitoring of competitive swimmers can provide important information when applied either after prescribed exercise or at rest for short- and long-term evaluation, respectively. Blood is the biological material most commonly used for such purposes, followed by urine. Lactate is widely measured in the context of exercise testing for the estimation of training intensity zones and the assessment of training adaptations. However, recent evidence and practice take the use and interpretation of lactate readings a step beyond the traditional views. Additionally, since the concept of adaptive homeostasis is being gradually embodied in biological sciences, we propose the use of additional biomarkers, like redox or inflammatory ones, for the evaluation of the adaptive ability of swimming training sets/sessions and of swimmers’ conditioning. Biochemical monitoring at rest is commonly used to estimate swimmers’ overall status, mainly in terms of the oxygen transport system, stress and fatigue levels, and nutritional status. Though the use of routine biochemical testing is widespread, focus on competitive swimmers is lacking. In the current commentary we aim to summarize and update the information on the utility of classical biomarkers and introduce potentially useful markers that can be added to the arsenal of biochemical monitoring of swimmers. Monitoring is essential for the advancement of health and performance of competitive athletes, and its successful use can facilitate training planning and produce significant performance gains. It should be individualized, orderly planned, and carefully applied. © 2018 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
