AB  - Aim. The aim was to quantify for the irst time the physical and physiological proile of professional soccer players in oficial games using GPS and heart rate (HR) response. Methods. Thirty professional soccer players were investigated during a half in competitive club level matches (N.=348) using GPS devices. Results. The relative total distance was 118.9±10.7 mmin-1 and player's Work-To-Rest Ratio was 2.1:1. Defenders covered the lowest total distance, while Second-Strikers (2ndS) and Wide- Midielders (W-MD) traveled the greatest total distance. Defenders presented the lowest Work-To-Rest Ratio values. Playing position also impacted on all sprinting performance results, except in average sprint distance and time of sprint. The number of sprints and repeated-sprint sequences recorded by the W-MD and Strikers (S) were signiicantly greater than any other group. The average HR recorded was 87.1%HRmax and the relationship between the external and internal load value (Efindex) was 1.4 with signiicant differences in both between playing positions. W-MD recorded a signiicantly smaller average HR than any other group and Centre-Backs showed a signiicantly smaller Efindex value than any other group. Conversely, W-MD showed a signiicantly greater Efindex value than any other group, except the 2ndS. Conclusion. This study has veriied a number of statistically signiicant differences between the different playing positions. Coaches should be focused on the speciic physical and physiological requirements of the playing positions to optimize the training prescription in soccer. The relationships between external and internal load measures among position-speciic indicates that players with less overall running performance during match-play were the worst in Efindex.
