AB  - Objective of the study was to analyse the effects of small doses of exogenous ammonium succinate on the cardiovascular and respiratory system functionality rates in rehabilitation period following aerobic training loads. Subject to the experiment were 22 highly skilled athletes tested in the aerobic ergometer practices. The subjects' cardiovascular and respiratory system functionalities were rated in the rehabilitation period following the aerobic practices. The onetime small doses of exogenous ammonium succinate were tested to increase (or keep the same) the rehabilitation process pace as verified by every cardiovascular and respiratory system functionality rate in the rehabilitation period following the aerobic practices even when the workloads were higher than usual. The external respiration rates in the rehabilitation period stayed the same upon administration of ammonium succinate. However, the oxygen demand rates were tested to recover faster in case of ammonium succinate administration versus the rehabilitation process non-assisted by ammonium succinate. On the 3rd minute of the rehabilitation process, lactate contents in blood were 15.5% different from the starting ones with the difference growing with the falling absolute values of lactate.
