TI  - Maximal aerobic frequency of ball hitting: A new training load parameter in tennis
PY  - 2017
T2  - Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research
VL  - 31
IS  - 1
SP  - 106
EP  - 114
DO  - 10.1519/JSC.0000000000001480
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85017511216?origin=resultslist
AB  - Baiget, E, Iglesias, X, and Rodríguez, FA. Maximal aerobic frequency of ball hitting: a new training load parameter in tennis. J Strength Cond Res 31(1): 106-114, 2017-This study aimed (a) to evaluate a new training load parameter in tennis based on the ball-hitting frequency (Ballf) at V O2max occurs (maximal aerobic frequency of ball hitting, MAF) and (b) to assess the accuracy of a specific endurance tennis test (SET-Test) for predicting MAF. Thirty-five male competitive tennis players performed the SET-Test and selected physiological and performance parameters at maximal workload (MAX), and last completed stage (LS) and MAF were compared. Performance parameters (Ballf, time, stage, and hits per test) at LS were higher than at MAF (20.2 ± 1.7 vs. 18.1 ± 1.5 shots min-1, 6.6 ± 0.8 vs. 5.6 ± 0.8 stages, and 189 ± 33 vs. 147 ± 27 hits; p , 0.001), and highly correlated (r = 0.7220.77; p < 0.001). The mean difference between Ballf and stage at MAF and LS were 2.1 6 1.1 shots min21 and 1.1 6 0.6 stages, respectively. The main physiological parameters (heart rate, VO2, and VCO2 at LS) were higher than at MAF (191 ± 9 vs. 186 ± 8 beats$min21, 55.5 ± 5.9 vs. 55.0 ± 6.0 ml$kg21$min21 and 4,724 ± 880 vs. 4,253 ± 739 ml$min21; p , 0.005), and were very strongly correlated (r = 0.9320.99; p , 0.001). We conclude that MAF can be used as a practical performance parameter to prescribe tennis-specific training, and that the SET-Test is a valid method for assessing MAF. Gas exchange measurements not being available, as a rule of thumb, most players reach their MAF at ∼ 1 stage (95% confidence interval: 0.921.2) and ∼ 2 shots$min21 (95% confidence interval: 1.722.5) less than their completed LS. A model for specific on-court training protocols for optimizing aerobic fitness in competitive tennis player is proposed. © 2015 National Strength and Conditioning Association.
