TI  - Information value of rhythmocardiographic characteristics in monitoring of training process
PY  - 2015
T2  - Teoriya i Praktika Fizicheskoy Kultury
IS  - 3
SP  - 17
EP  - 19
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84929011661?origin=resultslist
AB  - Monitoring of the training process by analyzing heart rate variability (HRV) using rhythmocardiography (RCG) is a very promising, successfully developing and modern trend in sports medicine, physiology and sport theory, an indispensable method for coaches and physicians. Today it is the most informative method for on-line monitoring of the functional state of athletes, much better than pulsometry diagnostics, which is traditionally used to control the exercise stress in sport. HRV is a method of analysis of sinus node automaticity, indicating the state of regulatory systems of the body and the degree of its balance with the internal and external environment. Inter-beat intervals in seconds are analyzed. The advantages of the method are that it is non-invasive, simple and easily done during preparation and in the process of research for both an athlete and a researcher. Its important features are: accessibility, short examination time, high sensitivity, computerized processing of raw data and the ability to be used during different test sample loads. The method of rhythmocardiography has been used in sports practice for over 30 years not only to estimate athlete's functional state, but to control the training process and to predict athlete's progress. The paper is devoted to the study of the information value of rhythmocardiographic indicators for athlete's assessment. Nowadays a researcher has to deal with at least 30 indicators when processing rhythmograms. To make the monitoring of the training process more convenient one is to have only a few indicators that could objectively and reliably proved athlete's functional state. Hence, the purpose of the present research was to make an objective search of the most informative indicators for the assessment of rhythmocardiography of an athlete. 90 highly skilled male and female athletes aged 17-24 who were engaged in endurance training in the pre-season were examined. The study was carried out using the computer analyzer "Cardiometer-MT" (Micard-Lana). The data file of 90 athletes in 19 examined indicators was statistically processed using the Excel software. According to the findings, out of 19 RCG indicators examined, the complex rhythmocardiographic characteristics proposed by R.M. Baevsky, being VRI and IARP, were proved to be the most informative for the athletes, engaged in the endurance training, in terms of showing objective information on the state of athlete's regulatory systems. From the indicators recommended by the International standard these are SDNN. dX and RMSSD.
