TI  - Influence of terminal rpe on session rpe
PY  - 2013
T2  - Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research
VL  - 27
IS  - 10
SP  - 2800
EP  - 2805
DO  - 10.1519/JSC.0b013e3182830d6c
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84888255150?origin=resultslist
AB  - Session rating of perceived exertion (RPE; SRPE), a convenient model for monitoring the overall perceived exertion of an exercise bout, is not well understood. The SRPE may be linked to final acute RPE before exercise cessation. This study investigated the potential link between terminal acute RPE (TRPE) and the SRPE using cycling trials of equated work. Fifteen subjects (age: 24.3 6 5.1 years) completed a maximal exertion cycle trial followed by 2 (counterbalanced) 40-minute cycling trials at approximately 75% of individualized V-O2max. By manipulating warm-up and cooldown, the trials were designed to result in a high TRPE (HITRPE) and low TRPE (LOTRPE). The heart rate (HR) and RPE were recorded every 5 minutes during exercise, with the SRPE recorded 20 minutes postexercise. The mean RPE (MeanRPE) during exercise (min 1040 HITRPE and minutes 535 for LOTRPE) was calculated by averaging all RPE responses recorded during exercise at 75% V-O2max. Two-way (trial 3 time) repeated measures analysis of variance and Bonferroni post hoc tests were used to compare the MeanRPE, SRPE, and TRPE. MeanRPE, HR, and power output (using paired t-test) for exercise at 75% V-O2max did not differ (p . 0.05) between HITRPE and LOTRPE. The TRPE at minute 40 was significantly lower (p , 0.05) for LOTRPE (3.1 6 2.6) vs. HITRPE (8.2 6 1.7), no significant difference was found for SRPE (LOTRPE: 7.6 6 2.0 vs. HITRPE 7.4 6 1.7). The findings indicate that the SRPE was not linked to TRPE. Further research is warranted using various modalities to extend the understanding of potential mediating factors of SRPE. © 2013 National Strength and Conditioning Association.
