UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84941143052?origin=resultslist
AB  - This experimental study using a non-equivalent control group aimed to establish the effectiveness of a breathing intervention for stress induced by exercise. The effect of respiratory sinus arrhythmia (RSA) training on the autonomic nervous system was analyzed in university students who were subjected to stress induced by excessive treadmill running. Thirty-eight healthy university students were allocated based on the results of a coin toss to an experiment group that would receive breathing training or a control group that would not receive the training. The participants were subjected to stress induced by use of a treadmill for 20 min after which those in the experiment group underwent breathing training using the RSA training program as visually presented on the computer monitor, while comfortably seated, for 10 min. The test results showed a significantly positive difference in the experiment group for sympathetic activity (t=3.88, p=.001), systolic blood pressure (t=5.25, p=<.001), stress resistance (t=−2.89, p=.009), stress index (t=4.98, p=<.001), and psychological stress (t=6.93, p=<.001). In conclusion, RSA training is an effective intervention for reducing stress induced by excessive exercise. © Research India Publications.
