TI  - Emerging concepts in exercise training for obese subjects: Exercise training in fasting condition
PY  - 2013
T2  - Exercise Therapy in Adult Individuals with Obesity
SP  - 269
EP  - 280
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84895249995?origin=resultslist
AB  - Increasing levels of obesity, arising from an energy-rich high-fat diet, in conjunction with sedentary lifestyle is largely driving the increasing incidence of insulin resistance (IR) and type 2 diabetes (T2DM) in Western populations. Exercise training is a cornerstone in the prevention of obesity and IR. However, time availability and/or motivation are often major barriers to spend many hours in exercise training, which highlights the need for "fine-tuning" of exercise training programs to obtain the most desirable outcomes in terms of prevention, as well as treatment, of obesity and IR. It is the current belief that muscular IR in obese individuals results, at least partly, from a persistent mismatch between fatty acid (FA) uptake and FA oxidation in skeletal muscle cells. Interestingly in this regard, the low circulating insulin level, versus elevated plasma adrenalin concentration associated with an acute exercise bout after an overnight fast, stimulates the rate of adipose tissue lipolysis and peripheral FA oxidation. Furthermore, it has been demonstrated that an endurance exercise training program in the fasted state is more potent than fed exercise training, with a similar exercise intensity and duration, to rescue whole-body glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity during an episode of hypercaloric high-fat diet in young healthy individuals. These beneficial effects resulting from regular exercise in the carbohydrate-restricted state seems to be due to muscular adaptations, which are believed to contribute to improved glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity. Still, the impact of endurance training in the fasted state is not yet investigated in obese subjects, although is tempting to speculate that this nutritional strategy may also facilitate exercise training adaptations in the latter population. Therefore, fasted exercise interventions may offer a novel strategy to include in societal intervention programs to prevent/treat glucose intolerance and deterioration of peripheral insulin sensitivity in obese populations. © 2013 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
