EP  - 28
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/20444469337?origin=resultslist
AB  - Chromium thin film layers with various grain sizes in the nanometer range were deposited by magnetron sputtering. The grain size of the layers was estimated using the full width of the half height (FWHH) of Cr (111) peaks in the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns. The microstructure and composition were analyzed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) equipped with energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS). The hardness of the layers was evaluated by Micro-Vickers indentation technique. The topography of the wear tracks was estimated using an atomic force microscope (AFM). A microtribometer with maximum external load of 50 mN and sapphire sphere (1 mm diameter) as counter sample was employed to study the tribological properties. The running speed was kept at less than 200 μm/s to obtain a quasistatic rubbing motion. This setup is deliberately designed to simulate a single asperity contact. Significant influence of grain size on friction and wear of Cr thin film layers was found and discussed with the microstructures and sputtering conditions.
