AB  - This study investigated the training potential of women in the seventh, eighth, and ninth decades of life. The data indicate that elderly women can improve functional capacity through a systematic program of low-intensity exercise. Exercising at either 40 or 60% of heart rate reserve was sufficient to induce gains in V̇O2max but did not significantly alter maximal heart rate, blood lactate concentrations, rate pressure product, or serum lipids. The data suggest that there is no evidence of a difference in degree of training response between moderate- and low-intensity exercise groups over a short period of time in population of women with very low aerobic capacity. Therefore, it is recommended that the lower intensity exercise prescription be considered for those groups that are at higher risk, particularly at the initiation of an exercise program where pre-exercise screening is not employed.
