AB  - The purpose of this investigation was to examine the daily heart rate responses of cardiac patients participating in a rehabilitation program. Sixteen male patients were monitored (Holter) for a 24-hour period that included an exercise session, an 8-hour workday, sleep, and various daily activities. The magnetic Holter tapes were analyzed on an electrocardioscanner for electrocardiographic abnormalities and heart rate intensities. Special attention was focused on the intensity and duration achieved by all subjects during the exercise session and the intensity of activity achieved during a typical workday and during other routine activities for the 24-hour monitoring period. Although patients maintained their heart rates within the prescribed target zone for only approximately 10 minutes of a 20-minute aerobic training phase, significant improvements in MET capacity (P < .05) were observed. Therefore, a training range of 40% to 60% of maximum heart rate reserve is sufficient to produce significant improvement in the functional capacity of select cardiac patients. © 1982 The American Burn Association.
