UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0028841611?origin=resultslist
AB  - Ten young swimmers (five boys, five girls: see table I) averaging 15 years in age were studied at rest then during recovery of maximal treadmill exercise (constant slope of 10% and increase of speed) several physiological parameters were studied: ventilatory flow (V̇E), heart rate (fc), oxygen consumption (V̇O2 and carbon dioxide production (V̇CO2 but also arterial pressure. Kinetics of these parameters were studied for 10 minutes considering time constants (τ) and total delay (Ttot) for complete recovery (see table II). Figure I shows that recovery has generally two components: the first is fast, (τl: about 2 min for V̇E, 2.5 min for fc, 1 min for V̇O2, 1.5 min for V̇CO2, the second is a slow one (τ2: about 6 min for V̇E, higher than 15 min for fc, 6 min for V̇O2, 5 min for V̇CO2). In these conditions, the calculated total recovery should be estimated within 30 min for V̇O2, 1 hour for fc, 30 min for V̇O2 and V̇CO2. Note that these calculated delays are higher than really observed, mainly for fc. Whatever the considered parameter, there exists a sexual difference: when compared to the girls, boys exhibit a faster first phase (lower τ1) and a slower second phase (higherτ2) : the majority of these differences are significant. In the same manner, V̇CO2 recovery is faster than V̇O2 recovery in girls but not in boys (table II). Moreover, whatever the sex or the phase, there exists an important difference in the kinetics of ventilation and cardiac recoveries: recovery of heart rate is much longer than for ventilatory flow as shown in table III (p < 0.05 or best). In fact, if after 2 minutes about 60% of recovery are observed for FC and V̇E, only 80% are observed for FC at 10 minutes in regards of 98% for V̇E at the same time. The boys and girls differences are discussed with reference to the sympathetic-parasympathetic balance of influences. Cardiovascular changes during exercise have a twofold control: decrease of vagal tone and increase of sympathetic activity. The comparison of boys and girls concerning cardiovascular data led to suppose that there exists a sexual difference in regards of the catecholaminergic sensitivity. The problem of thermoregulation must not be neglected during recovery. As swimmers are considered, cutaneous thermolysis is perhaps important when these subjects perform exercise in ambient air and not in water. Here again, it is known that thermolysis mechanisms are not similar in boys and girls. © 1995 Elsevier, Paris.
