UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0019860410?origin=resultslist
AB  - The training response of eleven Canadian National swimmers (six males and five females) was investigated. These swimmers were tested before and after a six-month training period for (1) maximal aerobic power (V̇O2max), (2) maximal heart rate, amd (3) blood lactate concentrations, using a free swimming test. The training consisted of a daily swimming distance ranging from 8,000 to 18,000 meters. V̇O2max increased from 4.11 to 4.44 l/min (P<0.05; 8% increase) and from 3.15 to 3.37 l/min (P<0.05; 7% increase) for male and female groups, respectively. Maximal heart rate decreased significantly (P<0.05) for both groups of swimmers (8.9 and 5.8 beats/min for male and female, respectively). It was concluded that cardiovascular adaptations can occur in elite athletes submitted to a severe training program.
