AB  - When a subject stands up quickly there is a change of heart rate and FCBF (Finger Capillary Blood Flow) as mediated through the autonomic nervous system. Changes occur in underlying control oscillations of breathing and baroreceptor responses and the transient shifts in the system may be quantified by using running spectral techniques. Normal young men show changes in the respiratory amplitude influence on HRV (Heart Rate Variability) and FCBF. Upon standing, there is a transient disturbance of the frequency of THM (Traube-Herrring-Mayer wave) which then settles at a higher power. These changes explain the differences in mean heart rate between the two positions. This approach to assessment of these changes in HRV and FCBF in response to sudden changes in HRV and FCBF in response to sudden standing may form the basis of a valuable clinical investigation of autonomic function.
