TI  - Evaluation of an existing and a new technique for the normalization of ground reaction forces: Total body weight versus lean body weight
PY  - 2000
T2  - Clinical Kinesiology
VL  - 54
IS  - 4
SP  - 90
EP  - 95
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0034578605?origin=resultslist
AB  - This experimental study examines the current procedure followed by most investigators for the normalization of ground reaction forces through the evaluation of total and lean body weight relationships, which could lead to the introduction of an alternative normalization technique. Twenty-four healthy male subjects volunteered to perform level walking and jogging. All subjects provided normal total (TBW) and lean body weights (LBW) ranging at 78 kg and 80%, respectively. Force data was recorded with the utilization of a force platform and specially designed software. LBW data was determined through hydrostatic weighing and skinfold measurements. A statistical analysis was performed, and it was found that although vertical ground reaction forces are mostly total weight dependent, researchers should consider utilizing lean weight normalizations since they have been indicated to be more applicable. Lean weight normalizations provide a different kind of information, which allows scientists to determine the body's response to external loading. Pearson's correlation coefficients verified this idea; they indicate that ground reaction force data is better represented when expressed as a coefficient percentage of lean body weight (correlation coefficient differences in favor of LMW reaching values of 0.2 to 0.3). It is necessary, however, to have more studies done in order to establish alternative standards for gait analysis. It is essential to have force data corresponding to higher speeds (genuine running conditions) collected and analyzed so that the behavior of muscle tissue can be examined in terms of force generation and absorption.
