AB  - Previous work has shown that when {Mathematical expression} kinetics are compared for endurance trained athletes and untrained subjects, the highly trained athletes have a faster response time. However, it remains to be determined whether the more rapid adjustment of {Mathematical expression} toward steady state in athletes is due to {Mathematical expression} differences or training adaptation alone. One approach to this problem is to study the time course of {Mathematical expression} kinetics at the onset of work in athletes who differ in {Mathematical expression} but have similar training habits. Therefore, the purpose of these experiments was to compare the time course of {Mathematical expression} kinetics at the onset of exercise in athletes with similar training routines but who differ in {Mathematical expression}. Ten subjects ( {Mathematical expression} range 50-70 ml · kg-1 · min-1) performed 6-minutes of cycle ergometer exercise at ∼50% {Mathematical expression}. Ventilation and gas exchange were monitored by open circuit techniques. The data were modeled with a single component exponential function incorporating a time delay, (T D ); {Mathematical expression}, where Τ is the time constant {Mathematical expression} is the increase in {Mathematical expression} at time t and {Mathematical expression} is the steady-rate increment above resting {Mathematical expression}. Kinetic analysis revealed a range of {Mathematical expression} half times from 21.6 to 36.0 s across subjects with a correlation coefficient of r=-0.80 (p<0.05) between {Mathematical expression} and {Mathematical expression} half time. These data suggest that in highly trained indicivuals with similar training habits, those with a higher {Mathematical expression} achieve a more rapid {Mathematical expression} adjustment at the onset of work. © 1985 Springer-Verlag.
