UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0027766243?origin=resultslist
AB  - The evaluation of heart rate modifications induced by training allows important information about physiological cardiac adaptations to sports activity. New methods regarding spectral analysis of R-R variability provide some information about sympathetic (LF) and parasympathetic (HF) activity controlling heart rate. Tilt Test is able to induce a strong stimulation of the sympatovagal system by means of a marked reduction of cardiac volume during upright posture. In our study this test has been carried out on a group of middle-aged athletes practising endurance (R) or power (B) sports activity, compared to sedentary subjects (S). Our results show an increase both in LF and even more in the HF component in trained athletes at rest. Moreover, the LF/HF ratio appears significantly lower in group R than in group B and S. It can suggest that sports activity could determine an upward resetting of the sympathovagal balance with relative greater vagal tone in endurance athletes. During upright posture we observed an increase of heart rate and a progressive sliding of balance toward sympathetic tone obtained by means of a prevalent withdrawal of vagal activity. Moreover, a greater incidence of syncope among endurance athletes was observed during upright posture.
