AB  - Prokhorov's hot cracking theory describes the conditions to express solidification cracking mathematically. these are the derivation of the internal deformation per temperature between the upper and lower temperatures for the existence of a liquid interdendritic phase between the solid state of dendrites. hot cracking appears when this deformation rate within the brittleness temperature range (btr) is higher than the critical deformation rate. most hot cracking tests are based on this theory. the pvr test (programmed deformation cracking test) quantitatively determines the solidification and liquation cracking sensitivity of base material and weld metal as one of the hot cracking tests with external loaded specimen. modelling of the pvr test is based on actual experimental testing conditions of a ni-base alloy. the temperature field. Deformation and stress field in the specimen are calculated. As they are simultaneously yielded and superimposed by the testing processes depending on time. a step forward in hot cracking assessment of weldment is done by calculating the critical deformation rate by prokhorov's theory. Using the experimental result of pvr test and btr-measurement with e.g. a gleeble as well as the fem-model.
