AB  - In this study, a series of methodical stress-strain analyses for the solder joints in surface-mount assembly (SMA) and ball grid array (BGA) assembly are carried out to investigate the elastic-plastic behavior, creep and stress relaxation behavior due to the thermal during temperature cycling or mechanical cyclic loading. The temperature dependent plastic behavior (yield stress) and time-temperature dependent behavior (creep properties) are taken into consideration in all numerical analyses. In order to get a cyclic strain hysteresis in steady-state in the solder joints during a temperature cyclic test, a simple analytic model is proposed. The analytic results show that for a temperature cyclic test, long high-temperature and low-temperature dwell times do not contribute to the cyclic strain range in solder joint, greatly. It is found that the strain range is affected by the total strain rate due to external loading in the stress concentration point. Based upon the results of the strain analyses, some efficient testing processes of temperature cycling and mechanical fatigue experiments for the microelectronics solder joint are proposed, and the cycling tests are carried out. The experimental results show a good agreement with the analytic results.
