AB  - Sixty subjects were tested for serum triglycerides, total cholesterol and lipoprotein fractions, before and after completing a 10 kilometer race. The population sample included groups of serious amateur and professional long and middle distance runners, police cadets, army reserve members and others. Fitness states overall varied from very unfit to extremely fit. Both male and female subjects were studied, and the time taken to complete the distance varied from 28 minutes to two hours. The results show increases in the mean levels of total cholesterol, alpha lipoproteins and beta lipoproteins with decreases in mean triglycerides and pre-beta lipoproteins. The established ischaemic heart disease risk ratios of total cholesterol to alpha lipoproteins and also beta lipoprotein to alpha lipoprotein ratio showed a similar decrease. It is suggested that the increase in alpha lipoprotein levels increased the circulating cholesterol by removing it from arterial walls and tissues. Other explanations are put forth. The changes appear to be independent of degree of fitness or the training status of the subject. Further studies 48 hours after the run showed that the elevation in circulating alpha lipoprotein appears to be transient and remains at post-run levels only if exercise is maintained, and had returned to pre-run levels, or less, in those subjects who undertook no exercise during the post-run period.
