TI  - Effects of training on the autonomic nervous system in young swimmers
ST  - FUNZIONE NEUROVEGETATIVA CARDIOVASCOLARE ED ALLENAMENTO IN GIOVANI NUOTATORI
PY  - 1995
T2  - International Journal of Sports Cardiology
VL  - 4
IS  - 2
SP  - 75
EP  - 79
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0029080894?origin=resultslist
AB  - The autonomic nervous system is of critical importance in the beat to beat regulation of heart rate and cardiac contraction. Continuous and daily training is capable of producing considerable changes in cardiocirculatory function probably due to modifications in the neural control, which are investigated with power spectral analysis of heart rate variability. This technique shows two main frequency components: high frequency and low frequency that can be used as markers of the vagal and sympathetic influences on the modulations of heart rate. The aim of this investigation was to evaluate the effects of training on the autonomic nervous system in 13 young swimmers (males, mean age 15 years, range 13-17) of the national team. They were studied for the duration of the training programme (almost 7 months). The sympatho-vagal interaction was assessed by power spectral analysis at rest, during tilting (head-up tilt test 70°) and after recovery. Each component was presented in normalized units. Student's t-test for paired data was used to determine the significance of the differences between the beginning, the middle, the final phase of training. No differences were observed at the beginning and middle phases, such as the response to a sympathetic stimulus induced by tilt, and there were no significant differences between the different phases of study. However, at the top of training, we found a significant increase in parasympathetic function at rest, while the sympathetic activity was higher after recovery compared to basal conditions. How the nervous system modulates cardiac performance and its interactions is complex. Our results suggest that both vagal and sympathetic control act within the physiologic range and that the gradual increase in neural activity in sportsmen is influenced by both components. More specifically, these data indicate that sympathetic predominance is detectable not only during tilting, but also in recovery conditions at the top of the athletic performance. The power spectral analysis of heart rate variability can provide adequate information on the adjustment of cardiovascular neural control during training.
