AB  - The training response of college aged females with high initial fitness levels was investigated. A collegiate female cross-country team which achieved a state championship was tested preceding and at the termination of a 3-mth competitive season for (1) maximal aerobic power and (2) cardiorespiratory function at the speed of their best current 3-mile run effort. The training consisted of a weekly average distance of 38.8 miles with 8.3% of that distance directed to interval training. Maximal oxygen uptake increased from 50.1 to 54.8 ml/kg/min (P < 0.01; 9.1% increase). Three-mile run times improved from 22.2 to 20.4 min (P < 0.01) in conjunction with an increase (P < 0.05) of heart rate, V̇(o2),V̇(E), R and blood lactate on the post-season submaximal paced tests. The percentage utilization of aerobic capacity during the 3-mile run remained unchanged (89%). It was concluded that short-term training increased endurance and aerobic capacity in these runners despite initial performances that were relatively advanced.
