AB  - Normal middle-aged men (N=37) were trained in a structured fitness program of four months' duration. The subjects were divided post hoc into a regular exercise group (n=24) and a control group (n=13). Restrictions for group classifications were established a priori; subjects in the exercise group attended at least 70% of the fitness program meetings and worked at not less than 60% V̇O2 max. Biochemical and physiological markers of training adaptation were found using the univariate t-test. Multivariate discriminant function analysis showed that the lipid profile variables (LDLC/HDLC and HDLC) were powerful discriminators between pre- and posttraining. The data support the notion that during moderate endurance training of middle-aged men lipoprotein-cholesterol ratio shifts occur, and several of the risk factors associated with having atherosclerotic heart disease are diminished.
