TI  - Heart rate response at the onset of dynamic exercise during cholinergic stimulation with pyridostigmine
PY  - 1997
T2  - FASEB Journal
VL  - 11
IS  - 3
SP  - A45
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/33750128718?origin=resultslist
AB  - Decreased cardiac parasympathetic activity is related to increased risk for sudden death after acute myocardial infarction (AMI), and it can be proposed that stimulation of the cardiac cholinergic receptors improve the prognosis of these patients. The purpose of this study was to determine the acute effect of pyridostigmine bromide (PYR) on the resting and reflex HR responses to dynamic exercise in healthy humans. Eight subjects [5 males; 3 females; age (mean±SD) 28±1 years] were submitted to a resting 12-lead electrocardiogram on the supine position and to a 4-sec bout of unloaded upright cycling (4-SET) on a mechanical ergometer. The HR response to the onset of exercise, which is mediated by vagal withdrawal, was quantified by the B/C ratio [last R-R interval before the onset of exercise (B) and the shortest one during cycling (C)]. On two (n=8) or three (n=4) different days following a randomized crossover double-blind protocol, the experiments were performed before and 120 min after oral administration of placebo (PLA), 30 mg (PYR30; n=8) or 45 mg of PYR (PYR45; n=4). The protocol was approved by the Institutional Review Board. Resting blood pressure was unchanged and PYR was well tolerated. Seric cholinesterasic activity was inhibited by 13% (n=3). Resting HR decreased after PYR (PYR30: 68±3 vs 61±3 bpm; P=0.003; PYR45: 61±3 vs 55±2 bpm; P=0.074), but not after PLA (66±3 vs 64±2 bpm; P=0.696), Although the R-R intervals during the 4-SET were increased (P<0.05), the reflex HR response did not change (B/C ratio: PLA; 1.53±0.07 vs 1.50±0.08; PYR30: 1.56±0.10 vs 1.54±0.09; PYR45: 1.48±0.20 vs 1.50±.024; P>0.05). We concluded that 30 mg of oral PYR produces tonic cardiac cholinergic stimulation and reduces resting HR, but causes no change in reflex vagal activity. Further studies should determine the effect of PYR in patients after AMI.
