AB  - Reported discrepancies and conflicting physiological principles as they relate to myocardial oxygen consumption (MVO2) in the mechanically bypassed left ventricle (LV) are analyzed. The residual mechanical energy generated in an unloaded LV is associated with a finite MVO2, stemming from myofibril cross-bridge cycling and basal metabolism. The existence of residual wall-stress, manifested as intramyocardial tissue pressure, implicates the existence of myocardial shortening against an internal load. The process of active unloading, by a mechanical pump, may in itself unfavorably alter the contractile pattern of the myocardium and thus the overall geometry of the LV chamber.
