AB  - This study was designed to investigate, whether heart rate (HR) at given running speeds or post-exercise heart rate recovery after maximal and submaximal efforts were good predictors of endurance capacity (EC), expressed as the running speed, at which a blood lactate concentration of 4 mmol/l occurred. The analysis was based on data of 313 incremental tests of Swiss elite sportsmen (age 22,9 years +/- 5,5 years) on the treadmill. Heart rate at submaximal running speed was highly significantly related to EC, with inverse correlations between r = -0,70 and r = -0,82 for the different running speeds. HR recovery after maximal effort did not show any correlation to EC for the periods of both the first 30 secs and the 90th to the 120th sec after the end of exercise, while HR recovery between the 30th and 90th sec was significantly related to HR recovery, though the association was weak in absolute terms (r = 0,46). The HR recovery during the breaks of 30 secs duration between the running steps was more closely related to EC (r = 0,54), and even better so (r = 0,67), if HR recovery was expressed as the proportion of the HR measured at the running velocity before the break. Nevertheless, the two post-exercise measurements of HR were disappointingly poor predictors of individual EC, because of wide interindividual variation and the flat slope of the association. Thus, our findings suggest that analysis of HR recovery after maximal and submaximal efforts provides little additional information on interindividual differences in EC, if HR at different submaximal running velocities is considered.
