AB  - Results of functional examinations are presented, which were obtained at graduated loading on a bicycle ergometer, in 69 boys, age 14 years. The boys were divided into groups, containing untrained subjects and boys, who had been trained in light athletics, swimming and sports gymnastics. Significant differences were found between the boys trained in light athletics and swimming and the untrained boys (the control-group), as far as the functional response is concerned. According to several indices, as for instance PWC170, maximum oxygen uptake and maximum minute ventilation, as well as the heart rate course at submaximal loads, an increasing fitness has been found in boys practising different sports activities. The increase depended on the type of the motional activities: the fitness was only slightly higher in subjects practising sports gymnastics, it was higher in light athletes and highest in swimmers. In comparison with an average population, the high physical fitness reached in individual indices in 14-year-old boys values, found usually in a youth population of higher age, or even in an adult population. Conditions, originating the training adaptation are discussed. Physical fitness of a subject may be well characterized by the value of PWC170 and by the value of the constant k. calculated from three heart rate values, obtained at three submaximal loads. Similarly, the fitness may be judged of on basis of a comparison of the dependence of the heart rate or minute ventilation on the oxygen uptake, at graduated load. © 1968 Springer-Verlag.
