UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0020071255?origin=resultslist
AB  - 44 top-class tennis players were put through ergometric tests on a bicycle and tread mill. 1. The heart size, oxygen pulse, maximum oxygen uptake and lactate levels showed favourable adaptation in these tennis players. Compared to average persons there was an increase in the aerobic capacity and endurance performance in the men and youths. The values reached by well-trained endurance athletes were not obtained. The spiroergometric values lie in the same region as those from sportsmen in other game sports. The performance ability of the female tennis players showed insufficient training adaptation. 2. The improvements in performance and regulatory range is insufficient, taking into account the high physical demands of present day top class tennis. Specialised endurance training with additional elements such as long-distance running and intensive interval training could achieve further adaptation. The optimal training load can be set using the threshold heart rate. Top class tennis players should carry out endurance training at a heart rate of between 160-170/min. 3. The spiroergometric tests, particularly those on the running board provide clear proof of the organic requirements of tennis players. Diagnoses of performance in tennis is however made more difficult by the fact that the performance ability in this sport depends very much upon the technical and tactical ability and general coordination and concentration. These factors cannot be objectively evaluate at present.
