AB  - The impact of aerobic fitness training on response to psychosocial stress was examined. Heart rate and self-reported response to stress was monitored in subjects before and after a 10-week aerobic training program, and compared with that of groups receiving anaerobic or no training. Fitness training failed to yield between-group differences on laboratory or self-report measures. However, the degree of improvement in aerobic fitness for subjects in the aerobic group tended to correlate with more rapid heart rate recovery following psychosocial stress. It is suggested that training programs may have to be longer, and subjects initially distressed, for improvement in aerobic fitness to produce more marked psychologic change.
