AB  - Bearings with asymmetrical gas feed are unsuitable for rotors of high-speed turbine machines in which the direction of the external load vector on the bearing varies with time (transport installations). This is because under certain operating conditions the direction of the external load vector and the reaction of the gas lubricating lay can coincide in sign which leads to 'direct' contact between journal and liner and to rapid bearing failure. To determine the influence of asymmetrical feed on the ultimate running speed of high-speed rotors with an external load vector that varies in direction and magnitude with time, experimental studies were made of the operation of bearings with several variants of asymmetrical lubricant gas feed compared with bearings with symmetrical feed. Analysis of the experimental data indicates that the use of asymmetrical gas blast in bearings with smooth liners causes a marked reduction in the ultimate rotor speed (frequency) omega //u//1//t; this is apparently because the lubricant layer has only a slight damping effect because of the influence of part of the liner in which there are no nozzles and also because of the low angular stiffness. The damping effect of the lubricant layer is increased by a cavity in the zone not occupied by the nozzles so that this action of asymmetrical blast is partially suppressed.
