AB  - Respiratory sinus arrhythmia (RSA), generally attributed to parasympathetic tone, was assessed in 8 triathletes (23.5 yrs) and 9 age matched controls through a 5 min recording of ECG, airflow (with a Fleisch head mounted on a mask) and end tidal CO2 during spontaneous breathing. RSA was estimated by (i) spectral analysis of R-R intervals using the standardhigh (HF : 0.15-0.4 Hz) and low (LF : 0.04-0.15 Hz) frequency spectral components and (ii) a new statistical method for RSA based on a breath-by-breath analysis of heart rate variability ; the RSA amplitude is quantified as the best sine wave fitted to heart rate changes throughout a breath. Heart Rate Breathing Rate HF LF/HF RSA Ampl. /min /min % % A 67.5±9.9 11.1*4.3 15.1±9.1 4.4±3.7 16.6±13.2 C 75.2±10.7 12.5±2.8 35.3±22.0 2.3±3.2 19.8±6.6 Breathing rate was similar in both groups but as expected, athletes demonstrated training bradycardia. However, neither spectral components nor statistical analysis of RSA showed any differences between groups. These results indicate that training bradycardia is not associated with a higher parasympathetic tone as reflected by RSA or the HF component of heart rate variability.
