SP  - 65
EP  - 70
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0030769275?origin=resultslist
AB  - Sinus bradycardia is a common finding in endurance athletes. Although it may genetically be determined, most studies indicate that training bradycardia is a biological adaptation resulting from chronic exercise. Many Authors have proposed that there are changes in activity pattern of the autonomic nervous system. Recently, heart rate spectral analysis (HRSA) has been shown to be a good non-invasive tool to estimate the autonomic nervous system activity. The aim of this study was to evaluate sympatho-vagal activity in endurance-trained athletes, all presenting with sinus bradycardia at rest. Thirty-two male endurance athletes (17 cyclists, 13 long-distance runners, 2 swimmers, age range 19-31 years) with resting sinus bradycardia (44.13 ± 4.61 b/min) were recruited. We performed a continuous ECG recording on which HRSA was computed. All subjects were evaluated at rest, during and 5 min after a cold presser test. The control group was formed of 21 sedentary male subjects aged 22 to 33 years. Because of subjective report of pain during the cold presser test, only the data of 30 athletes and 16 control subjects were analyzed. At rest low (LF: 0.04-0.15 Hz) and high frequency (HF: 0.15-0.40 Hz) components were significantly different between endurance athletes and controls, with a LF/HF ratio significantly lower in endurance athletes than in controls (0.59 ± 0.08 vs 1.01 ± 0.06 nu, p < 0.005). The cold presser test results allowed us to divide the athletes into two groups: 1) in Group A (18 athletes) LF showed a significant increase while HF showed a decrease, that is, a pattern of behaviour qualitatively comparable to that of controls; 2) in Group B (12 athletes) LF did not show any significant change while HF showed a significant decrease (p < 0.001). However, in all subjects LF/HF ratio showed a significant increase (p < 0.005). During recovery, LF and HF returned to the basal conditions within 5 min only in endurance athletes (both Group A and Group B). In conclusion, HRSA reveals a different resetting of the autonomic nervous system in endurance athletes, supporting the hypothesis that training bradycardia could be due both to neural and non-neural mechanisms, whose relative contribution could be related to genetic factors.
