EP  - 265
DO  - 10.1016/0003-9861(82)90303-4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0020368433?origin=resultslist
AB  - The biochemical and physiologic integration of cellular and whole-animal energetics has been studied following sprint exercise training. Our previous studies with endurance training (K. J. A. Davies, L. Packer, and G. A. Brooks, 1981, Arch. Biochem. Biophys. 209, 539-554) led us to propose that muscle oxidative capacity is the primary determinant of endurance, but that whole-animal maximal O2 consumption (O2max) is not limited by muscle oxidative capacity, and is, therefore, an unreliable predictor of endurance. In the present study, O2max during exercise was increased by 15% (relative to controls) following a 4-week program of daily treadmill running at high speeds (similar to the 14% increase in O2max following endurance training). In contrast to endurance training, however, sprint training resulted in increased (10%) CO2 production at O2max, indicating that no shift to greater lipid utilization had occurred. Furthermore, sprint training did not affect muscle pyruvate oxidase, palmitoyl-carnitine oxidase, succinate oxidase, or cytochrome oxidase activities. Muscle cytochrome c (c1), and cytochrome a concentrations, as well as the mitochondrial content of muscle were also not increased by sprint training. These results are very different from endurance training adaptations which include 100% increases in muscle oxidase activities, and cytochrome and mitochondrial contents. Although the maximum speed which could be attained at O2max was increased 25% by sprint training, no improvement in endurance capacity was observed. The results demonstrate that it is possible to increase O2max with no change in muscle oxidase activities or endurance capacity. This dissociation is further evidence that O2max is not limited by muscle oxidase activity, but that endurance capacity and muscle oxidative capacity are closely coupled. © 1982.
