TI  - Future research on the quantifying of endurance training
PY  - 1974
T2  - Journal of Human Ergology
VL  - 3
IS  - 2
SP  - 163
EP  - 181
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0016349081?origin=resultslist
AB  - Current information stresses the importance of initial fitness and intensity of effort as determinants of the response to endurance training. Nevertheless, there is wide disagreement on the optimum combination of intensity, duration, and frequency of effort for a training regimen. It is suggested that one reason for apparently conflicting results is that the training response bears a complex, non linear relationship to each of these three variables. Ultimately training may be quantified in terms of the release of one or more 'training substances'. Possible candidate substances are reviewed. Currently, assessment must be in terms of physiological responses such as gains of aerobic power. Even if interest is restricted to one group (such as healthy young men), the possible combinations of intensity, duration and frequency are legion. While individual investigators can make ad hoc decisions on the merits of specific programmes, a full definition of response curves will require study of a very large population, making a 'multi centre' trial a necessity. When the response of young men has been established, investigations should be extended to other groups, including children, older subjects, women and patients suffering from a variety of disease conditions.
