TI  - Skeletal Muscle Limits the Exercise Tolerance of Renal Transplant Recipients: Effects of a Graded Exercise Training Program
PY  - 1990
T2  - American Journal of Kidney Diseases
VL  - 16
IS  - 1
SP  - 57
EP  - 65
DO  - 10.1016/S0272-6386(12)80786-4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0024998822?origin=resultslist
AB  - Sixteen renal transplant recipients were studied before and after they had participated in a 24-week exercise training program to determine (1) the nature of the factors explaining their impaired exercise tolerance, and (2) their adaptative responses to exercise training. During progressive treadmill exercise to exhaustion prior to training, renal transplant recipients stopped exercising at lower peak rates of oxygen consumption ( V˙ O2max) (29.0 ± 7.8 v 47.9 ± 9.1 mL O2·kg−1·min−1; P< 0.0001) and ventilation (55.9 ± 13.2 v 124.0 ± 22.2 L·min−1; P< 0.0001), and at lower peak heart rates (169 ± 22 v 196 ± 9 beats·min−1; P<0.05) and peak blood lactate concentrations (5.0 ± 2.1 v 11.5 ± 4.0 mmol·L−1; P<0.001) than did controls. None showed a plateau in oxygen consumption with increasing workload. Exercise time to exhaustion was also significantly shorter in renal transplant recipients (9.5 ± 1.8 v 16.0 ± 1.3 min; P<0.0001). After training, exercise time to exhaustion (12.0 ± 2.0 min; P<0.001),  V˙ O2max (37.5 ± 4.8 mL O2·kg−1·min−1; P<0.05), maximum ventilation rate (68.5 ± 14.0 L·min−1; P<0.05), peak blood lactate concentrations (7.8 ± 1.8 mmol·L−1; P<0.001), and the rate of oxygen consumption at a blood lactate concentration of 2.0 mmol·L−1 (22.5 ± 2.5 v 16.5 ± 2.2 mL O2·kg−1·min−1; P<0.001) had all increased significantly. In addition, there were large increases in isokinetic muscle function in both the quadriceps and hamstring muscles (P<0.001 to P<0.0001). However, most of these values were lower than those measured in untrained controls. Skeletal muscle biopsies performed after training in the six fastest runners in the group showed an unexpected preponderance of type II muscle fibers (67% ± 11%) and low oxidative capacity ( Q O2; 1,278 ± 358 μL O2·h−1·g−1). The study shows that the exercise tolerance of renal transplant recipients before training is limited by skeletal muscle factors. The primary adaptation to training would appear to be enhanced skeletal muscle contractile function. Although the magnitude of the training adaptations in renal transplant recipients is similar to that of nondiseased individuals, even after training, the exercise capacity of renal transplant recipients remains subnormal. Additional beneficial training adaptations included a reduction in resting heart rate, a reduced usage of medications to control blood pressure, and evidence for enhanced bone remodeling. © 1990, National Kidney Foundation, Inc.. All rights reserved.
