TI  - Perturbations in sleep and wakefulness rhythm during a marathon run
ST  - Perturbations du rythme veille-sommeil lors d'un raid d'endurance
PY  - 1989
T2  - Science and Sports
VL  - 4
IS  - 2
SP  - 101
EP  - 114
DO  - 10.1016/S0765-1597(89)80043-7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0024375353?origin=resultslist
AB  - Sleep/physical exercise relationship in man have been extensively studied for sportsmen, as sleep disorders significantly affect exercise. Results, however, are very different, depending on the extent of sleep deprivation and on the intensity of exercise. During a relay race this disturbance does not appear as clearly; several factors are interconnected sometimes with great complexity. A relay race is run over several days, offering the opportunity to study fractioned rest and rest periods shifted along the nychthemeron. Results of a study of disorders in the wake/sleep rhythm observed during a relay race are reported here: twenty-four subjects had to run 2.328 km over eight days and eight nights, i.e. more than 192 km per subject. The test was a "paper-pencil" type test using two types of questionnaires. The first called "departure questionnaire" (Fig. 1) evidenced the main medico-physiological characteristics of the subjects. The second, called "daily questionnaire" (Fig. 2) and the sleep log (Fig. 3), shows the day by day changes in a number of subjective parameters on sleep, fatigue and mood. A precise study of the qualitative aspect of sleep may, even more than the quantitative study, only yield valuable results if an EEG recording is made; however, this first approach based on the data extracted from questionnaires should evidence the biggest changes. Data provided by questionnaires and observations show that this physical exercise, although high-level, did not exhaust the runners. Organization and sometimes significant shifts of rest periods for certain teams induced in all runners a 25-30% decrease in sleep time compared to their usual sleep time (Table III). It thus appeared that the sleep schedule plays an important role regarding the quantity of sleep. The better this schedule corresponds to external conditions (lighting, social habits, etc.) and to biological rhythms, the closer the duration of sleep will be to the usual sleep duration. It also appears that team leaders usually slept less than their team-mates. A sleep deficit thus accumulated, expressed by increased positive responses relative to "drowsiness" during the race (Table V). However, this race was well accepted by all runners. Its somewhat unusual nature, the high-level and especially the long-term effort requested from the runners made the run a positive contest, with increased feelings of happiness and reduced subjective fatigue as the race went on. Changes in body weight, either increase or decrease, are relatively moderate and reflect a fair tolerance of the body to the requested effort. It should be noted that the two runners who had to drop out of the race were among those who put on the most weight (+3 kg and +2 kg, respectively). As a conclusion, the study of the wake-sleep rhythm during a long-time relay race, in spite of the limits of its methodology, and the subjective aspect of answers, provided a number of informations: 1) Regarding the impact of the consented effort: the physical trial did not exhaust runners. A sleep debt built up inducing a general feeling of drowsiness and the appearance, during the race, of short periods of sleep during transfers in vehicles. The amount of time to fall asleep and dreaming activity increased. 2) No observation reflects a qualitative differentiation between night-time and daytime sleep periods. However, when runners were given a choice, they showed a quantitative preference for night-time sleep. © 1989 Editions Scientifiques Elsevier.
