AB  - To study the internal phosphorus load in the Bay of Quinte, Lake Ontario, dialysis chambers were set in an array mounted on a platform at the sediment/water interface. Two sets of arrays were used, one set was sampled weekly and the other bi-weekly. Chamber samples were analysed for total phosphorus and dissolved oxygen, and temperature and pH were measured. Up-facing array tubes also acted as settling traps and, from these, quantities of trapped material and contents of organic carbon and organic nitrogen were measured. Sediment samples were recovered at the array sites, and temperature, pH, Eh, color, and near surface layer structures were recorded. The arrays were used to estimate proportions of the internal phosphorus load derived from degradation of fresh particulates, at or near the base of the water column, and partly aged bed material. Phosphorus released from partly aged lake bed sediment provided about 20 percent of the internal load at Hay Bay, but the bed acted as a sink for much of the time at Picton. At Hay Bay, sediment oxygen consumption rates were high, averaging more than 0.6 g 02 m−2 d−1. Higher oxygen consumption rates characterized Picton sediments during the late summer and early fall, and these were accompanied by a slight increase in the rate of phosphorus release. Combined mean P-reflux values derived from these experiments were higher than expected from model predictions based on past Bay of Quinte studies. The higher values may be caused by very rapid degradation of trapped material in the up-facing array tubes due to the presence of excessive numbers of benthic invertebrates. © 1990, International Association for Great Lakes Research. All rights reserved.
