AB  - Thermal fatigue tests of an unidirectional SiC fiber reinforced Ti-24Al-11Nb matrix composite, SCS-6/Ti-24Al-11Nb, have been carried out without external load in air and in vacuum, under various thermal cycle conditions. The fiber employed was a β-SiC fiber of 140 μm diameter with a carbon rich graded silicon carbide coating on the surface: SCS-6. This paper covers the following characteristics of the material subjected to thermal fatigue, compared with those subjected to long term isothermal exposure: (i) change of the visible crack density with thermal cycling; (ii) change of the fiber/matrix interface morphology by means of EPMA and SEM; (iii) change of the interfacial shear strength by the push-out tests, and (iv) effect of environment on the properties from (i) to (iii). Based on these systematic investigations, special attentions are paid to understand the mechanisms and mechanics of thermal fatigue failure, and how thermal fatigue damage should be assessed.
