UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0017206362?origin=resultslist
AB  - 7 members of the Aged Runners Association (6 men and 1 woman aged 59-75 yr) who had practiced almost daily distance runs of more than 10 km were compared to a control group of 23 untrained men of a similar age and 13 healthy adults aged 22-30 yr to determine the response of circulatory function to hydrostatic stress caused by passive postural change. Observations were made on blood pressure, R-R intervals in ECG and sphygmograms at the finger tip and ear lobe. In postural change of the upper half of the body, the systolic blood pressure was generally higher in the supine position than in the sitting position. Systolic pressure showed a transient decrease just after the passive postural change from reclining to sitting, and gradually recovered the resting level. This occurrence is well known and termed the postural blood pressure reflex. R-R intervals shortened just after postural change and recovered the resting level in about 1 minute. The amplitude of sphygmograms at the finger tip and ear lobe was greater in the supine position than in the sitting position; it was markedly reduced by erecting the upper half body from laying. This trend was rather common to the trained aged, the untrained aged and the young men. However, the magnitude of the change was generally greater in the young men. A decline in systolic blood pressure due to passive postural change was slight in the trained aged. This tendency was more marked when compared to the young men. Contrarily, changes in systolic blood pressure were generally far greater among the untrained aged and presented conspicuous individual differences. A general tendency was noted in that the slighter the transient decline of systolic pressure after the postural change, the faster its recovery to the resting level. However, no noticeable difference could be found among three groups in the relation between the transient decline of systolic pressure after postural change and systolic pressure 2 minutes after the postural change. A slight change of systolic blood pressure in response to passive postural change suggested superior regulatory function of blood pressure. The types of blood pressure changes due to passive postural change could be classified into several categories. Most of the trained aged were concentrated among the type with good regulatory function, as in the case of the young men. Regarding the untrained aged, they were dispersed into various types. The findings seem to indicate that aged persons trained by daily distance running show superior regulatory function of blood pressure in the response of circulatory functions to hydrostatic stress caused by passive postural change.
