AB  - Maximal oxygen uptake, respiratory quotient, maximal heart rate and lactate production in heavy ergometer loading were measured from three highly trained male endurance athletes and from three young students, whose habitual physical activity was moderate. Glycogen content, succinate dehydrogenase activity, muscle fiber composition, and relative mitochondrial volume were also estimated from needle biopsy samples of m. vastus lateralis at rest and after strenuous, exhaustive bicycle ergometer work. Generally, considerable compatibility was noticed between physiological variables and ultrastructural, biochemical and histological characteristics of the muscle samples. These observations show that certain variables in skeletal muscle correspond well with the degree of the subject's physical fitness (estimated as maximal oxygen uptake) and they can therefore be used in estimating the subject's condition. Some aspects of training adaptations at physiological and muscle cell level are briefly discussed.
