AB  - The transformation strain and reversible shape memory (RSM) of specimens made of the alloy TiNi with equiatomic composition subjected to preliminary flexural strain in the dynamic and quasi-statical velocity range were studied as affected by the external stresses, acting under heat cycling through the integrals of martensite transformations. It is shown that RSM evoked by the high-speed loading is more resistant to the effect of external stresses and is less than plasticity of direct martensite transformation, if the external loadings are oriented in such a way that they tend to suppress the RSM.
